export default [{"title": "Aloe Vera for Sunburn and Skin: How to Use It Safely", "excerpt": "Aloe vera gel feels soothing on sunburn and may help minor burns heal, but it won't undo sun damage. Here's how to use it, what to avoid, and when a burn needs medical care.", "category": "everyday-wellness", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Some evidence suggests aloe gel may help minor burns heal, but trials on sunburn itself have not shown clear benefit over other moisturisers; oral aloe latex is unsafe.", "tags": "aloe vera, sunburn, skin care", "slug": "aloe-vera-for-sunburn-and-skin-how-to-use-it-safely", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nAloe vera gel is a cooling, soothing moisturiser that many people find comfortable on mild sunburn. Dermatologists include moisturisers containing aloe in their sunburn care advice. However, studies have not shown that aloe makes sunburn heal faster or hurt less than other approaches, and nothing applied afterwards reverses the skin damage caused by UV exposure.\n\nAloe gel on the skin is safe for most people. Aloe taken by mouth, especially products containing the yellow **aloe latex**, is a different matter: it is a strong laxative and has been linked to serious side effects.\n\n## What aloe vera is\n\n*Aloe vera* is a succulent plant whose thick leaves contain two different substances:\n\n- **Aloe gel:** the clear, jelly-like inner part of the leaf. It is mostly water with sugars, vitamins and other compounds, and is used on the skin.\n- **Aloe latex:** a yellow sap just under the leaf skin, containing compounds called anthraquinones (such as aloin). It is a powerful laxative.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Minor burns\n\nA systematic review of burn studies found that aloe vera may help superficial and partial-thickness burns heal a few days faster than some comparison treatments. Most of these studies were small and of limited quality, and they involved minor burns treated under medical supervision rather than sunburn at home.\n\n### Sunburn\n\nThe evidence for sunburn specifically is disappointing. Small trials testing aloe gel after UV exposure found little or no difference compared with placebo gels in redness or recovery. Applying aloe *before* sun exposure does not prevent sunburn.\n\nThat does not mean aloe is useless. Cool gel can feel soothing and helps keep skin moisturised, which is an important part of sunburn care. It simply works as a comforting moisturiser, not as a treatment that speeds healing.\n\n### Other skin uses\n\nAloe is used for mild skin irritation, dry skin and minor wounds. Some small studies suggest benefits for conditions such as mild psoriasis or mouth ulcers, but evidence is limited.\n\n## How to use aloe vera on sunburn\n\nDermatologists recommend these steps for sunburn, and aloe fits into them:\n\n1. **Get out of the sun** and cool the skin with a cool (not icy) shower, bath or damp cloth for 10 to 15 minutes.\n2. **Apply a gentle moisturiser** while the skin is still slightly damp. A plain aloe vera gel or a moisturiser containing aloe or soy works well. Keeping the gel in the fridge makes it more soothing.\n3. **Take a pain reliever** such as ibuprofen or paracetamol if needed, following the label, to ease pain and swelling.\n4. **Drink extra fluids.** Sunburn draws fluid to the skin surface.\n5. **Leave blisters alone.** They protect the skin underneath. Do not pop them.\n6. **Cover up** with loose, tightly woven clothing and stay out of the sun while the skin heals.\n\nWhen choosing a product:\n\n- Look for a gel with a **high aloe content and few added ingredients**.\n- **Avoid products with \"-caine\" ingredients** (such as benzocaine or lidocaine), which can irritate skin or cause allergic reactions on burned skin.\n- **Avoid added fragrance and alcohol**, which can sting and dry the skin.\n- If using a fresh leaf, rinse it, cut it open and scoop out only the clear gel. Avoid the yellow latex layer, which can irritate skin.\n\n> Do a patch test first: apply a little gel to a small area of unburned skin and wait 24 hours. Stop using it if you notice redness, itching or a rash.\n\n## Side effects and cautions\n\n**On the skin**, aloe gel rarely causes problems. Some people develop allergic contact dermatitis (an itchy rash), particularly those allergic to plants in the lily family, such as garlic, onions and tulips.\n\n**By mouth**, aloe is not recommended:\n\n- **Aloe latex** causes cramps and diarrhoea and can lead to low potassium and dehydration. US regulators banned it from over-the-counter laxatives in 2002 due to lack of safety data.\n- **Whole-leaf aloe extract** that has not had the anthraquinones removed caused intestinal tumours in rats in a US government study.\n- There are case reports of **liver injury** linked to oral aloe products.\n- Oral aloe may lower blood sugar and interact with diabetes medicines, and low potassium from aloe latex can increase the risk of side effects from digoxin.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People allergic to aloe or lily-family plants (skin use)\n- Pregnant or breastfeeding women, children, and people with bowel or kidney conditions should not take aloe by mouth\n- Anyone taking digoxin, diuretics or diabetes medicines should avoid oral aloe\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSunburn can be a real burn. Seek medical help if:\n\n- Blisters cover a **large area** (for example, most of the back) or appear on the face, hands or genitals\n- You have **fever, chills, headache, confusion, nausea or vomiting** with sunburn, which may be sun poisoning or heat illness\n- You feel **faint or dizzy**, or show signs of dehydration\n- Signs of **infection** appear: increasing pain, swelling, warmth, pus or red streaks\n- A **baby under one year old** is sunburned\n- The burn is not improving after a few days\n\nAlso see a doctor or dermatologist about any **new or changing mole or skin spot**. Repeated sunburn raises the risk of skin cancer, including melanoma.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is fresh aloe better than store-bought gel?\n\nFresh gel is fine if you avoid the yellow latex. Commercial gels are more convenient and consistent, but check the ingredients for alcohol, fragrance and numbing agents.\n\n### Can aloe prevent peeling?\n\nKeeping skin moisturised may make peeling more comfortable, but it will not stop it. Peeling is the skin shedding damaged cells.\n\n### Can I use aloe vera on my face every day?\n\nMost people can use plain aloe gel as a light moisturiser. Stop if you notice irritation.\n\n### Does aloe help with tanning?\n\nNo. A tan is a sign of skin damage from UV light. Aloe does not change how you tan or protect you from burning.\n\n## References\n\n- American Academy of Dermatology. How to treat sunburn. https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/injured-skin/burns/treat-sunburn\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Aloe vera. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera\n- Maenthaisong R, et al. The efficacy of aloe vera used for burn wound healing: a systematic review. *Burns*, 2007.\n- US National Toxicology Program. Toxicology and carcinogenesis studies of a nondecolorized whole leaf extract of Aloe barbadensis Miller (aloe vera) in rats and mice, 2013.\n"}, {"title": "Apple Cider Vinegar: Benefits, Risks and Myths", "excerpt": "Apple cider vinegar is promoted for weight loss, blood sugar and \"detox\". The evidence is thin for most claims, and it can damage teeth and the throat if used carelessly.", "category": "heart-and-metabolism", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Small, short studies suggest vinegar with a meal may slightly lower the blood sugar rise after eating; evidence for weight loss is weak, and \"detox\" claims have no basis.", "tags": "apple cider vinegar, blood sugar, weight", "slug": "apple-cider-vinegar-benefits-risks-and-myths", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nApple cider vinegar (ACV) is a sour, acidic liquid made by fermenting apple juice. Small studies suggest that taking a little vinegar with a carbohydrate-rich meal may **slightly reduce the rise in blood sugar** afterwards. Evidence that it helps with weight loss is weak, and claims that it detoxifies the body, cures acid reflux or balances the body's pH are not supported.\n\nThe bigger issue is safety. ACV is acidic enough to **erode tooth enamel** and, if taken undiluted or as tablets, to injure the throat. If you choose to use it, dilute it well and protect your teeth.\n\n## What is in apple cider vinegar\n\nACV is mostly water and **acetic acid** (typically 4 to 6%), with small amounts of other acids, a little potassium and trace polyphenols. Unfiltered ACV contains \"the mother\", a cloudy mix of yeast and bacteria. It is sometimes marketed as a probiotic, but there is no good evidence it offers meaningful health benefits.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Blood sugar\n\nThis is the most studied area. Small trials, mostly lasting hours to a few weeks, have found that vinegar taken just before or with a high-carbohydrate meal can reduce the post-meal rise in blood glucose and insulin. Acetic acid appears to slow stomach emptying and may improve how muscles take up glucose.\n\nThe effect is modest, studies are small, and long-term effects on HbA1c (a measure of average blood sugar over months) are uncertain. Vinegar is not a treatment for diabetes and must not replace prescribed medicines.\n\n### Weight loss\n\nThe most cited study is a 12-week Japanese trial in which people who drank vinegar daily lost about 1 to 2 kg more than those on placebo. A few other small studies suggest similar small effects, possibly because vinegar makes people feel slightly fuller or a little queasy. The evidence is weak, and any effect is small compared with changes in diet and activity.\n\n### Cholesterol and blood pressure\n\nSome small trials report slight improvements in cholesterol or triglycerides, but results are inconsistent and many studies have methodological problems.\n\n### Myths without evidence\n\n- **\"Detox\":** your liver and kidneys clear waste products; vinegar does not help them do this.\n- **\"Alkalising\":** vinegar does not change your blood pH, which the body keeps in a tight range.\n- **Acid reflux:** there is no good evidence ACV helps reflux, and its acidity may make symptoms worse.\n- **Skin and hair:** applying undiluted ACV to skin can cause chemical burns. It is not a treatment for warts, moles or skin infections.\n\n## How people use it\n\nIf you want to try ACV, the safer way is:\n\n1. **Dilute it.** Use 1 to 2 teaspoons (5 to 10 ml), up to 1 tablespoon (15 ml), in a large glass (about 250 ml) of water.\n2. **Take it with a meal**, not on an empty stomach.\n3. **Use a straw** to reduce contact with teeth, and rinse your mouth with plain water afterwards.\n4. **Don't brush straight away.** Acid softens enamel for a while; wait at least 30 to 60 minutes before brushing.\n5. **Use it in food.** Salad dressings and marinades are an easy way to include vinegar with meals.\n\nAvoid ACV tablets and gummies. Tablets have caused throat injuries when they got stuck, the amount of acetic acid varies widely, and gummies add sugar.\n\n> From a dental point of view, sipping diluted vinegar throughout the day is the riskiest habit. Repeated acid exposure is what wears enamel away, and lost enamel does not grow back.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n- **Tooth erosion.** Acetic acid can dissolve enamel, leading to sensitivity, discolouration and a higher risk of decay.\n- **Throat and stomach irritation.** Undiluted vinegar can burn the throat and oesophagus and worsen indigestion.\n- **Delayed stomach emptying.** Helpful for blood sugar in theory, but a problem for people with gastroparesis, a condition common in long-standing diabetes.\n- **Low potassium.** There is a case report of very low potassium and bone loss after years of drinking large amounts.\n- **Medicine interactions.** ACV may add to the effects of insulin and other diabetes medicines (risk of low blood sugar), and may worsen low potassium caused by some diuretics or digoxin.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People with **gastroparesis**, reflux or stomach ulcers\n- People with **sensitive teeth or enamel erosion**\n- Anyone taking **insulin, sulfonylureas, diuretics or digoxin**, unless their doctor agrees\n- People with **kidney disease**\n- Children\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You are trying to manage **blood sugar, weight, cholesterol or blood pressure**. These conditions deserve proper assessment and proven treatment.\n- You have symptoms of **high blood sugar**: being very thirsty, passing a lot of urine, tiredness, blurred vision or unexplained weight loss\n- You have **pain when swallowing** or chest pain after taking vinegar or tablets\n- You have **persistent heartburn**, difficulty swallowing or vomiting\n- You develop **tooth sensitivity** or notice your teeth looking more yellow or translucent at the edges; see your dentist\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### When is the best time to take apple cider vinegar?\n\nIn studies on blood sugar, vinegar was taken just before or with a carbohydrate-containing meal. Taking it on an empty stomach first thing in the morning is more likely to cause irritation.\n\n### Does apple cider vinegar burn belly fat?\n\nNo. There is no evidence that it targets belly fat. Any weight effect in studies was small.\n\n### Is ACV with \"the mother\" better?\n\nThere is no good evidence that unfiltered vinegar with the mother is healthier than filtered vinegar.\n\n### Can I drink apple cider vinegar every day?\n\nSmall, well-diluted amounts with meals are unlikely to cause harm for most healthy adults, as long as you protect your teeth. There is no strong reason to take it daily for health.\n\n## References\n\n- Johnston CS, Kim CM, Buller AJ. Vinegar improves insulin sensitivity to a high-carbohydrate meal in subjects with insulin resistance or type 2 diabetes. *Diabetes Care*, 2004.\n- Kondo T, et al. Vinegar intake reduces body weight, body fat mass, and serum triglyceride levels in obese Japanese subjects. *Bioscience, Biotechnology, and Biochemistry*, 2009.\n- Hill LL, et al. Esophageal injury by apple cider vinegar tablets and subsequent evaluation of products. *Journal of the American Dietetic Association*, 2005.\n- Zero DT, Lussi A. Erosion: chemical and biological factors of importance to the dental practitioner. *International Dental Journal*, 2005.\n"}, {"title": "Chamomile Tea for Sleep: Does It Really Work?", "excerpt": "Chamomile is the classic bedtime tea. Small studies suggest it may improve how well people feel they slept, but it is not a treatment for insomnia. Here is what to expect.", "category": "sleep-and-stress", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "A handful of small trials suggest chamomile may modestly improve self-reported sleep quality and mild anxiety, but it has not been shown to treat insomnia.", "tags": "chamomile, sleep, herbal tea", "slug": "chamomile-tea-for-sleep-does-it-really-work", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nChamomile tea is a gentle, low-risk drink that many people find relaxing before bed. A few small studies suggest it may improve how well people *feel* they slept, and there is some evidence it eases mild anxiety. It has not been shown to help people fall asleep faster or stay asleep longer in a measurable way, and it is not a treatment for chronic insomnia.\n\nIf a warm cup of chamomile is part of a calm bedtime routine that helps you wind down, it is a reasonable thing to try. Just keep your expectations modest.\n\n## What chamomile is\n\nChamomile tea is usually made from the dried flowers of German chamomile (*Matricaria chamomilla*), a daisy-like plant. Roman chamomile is a related plant used less often. The flowers contain several active compounds, and one of them, **apigenin**, attaches to the same brain receptors (benzodiazepine sites on GABA-A receptors) that some sleep and anti-anxiety medicines act on, although much more weakly. That is the most common explanation for chamomile's calming reputation.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Sleep\n\nThe research on chamomile and sleep is small and mixed:\n\n- A 2019 systematic review in *Phytotherapy Research* pooled the few randomised trials available. Chamomile improved **sleep quality scores** compared with control groups, but it made no significant difference to standard measures of **insomnia**.\n- One trial in women who had recently given birth found that drinking chamomile tea daily for two weeks improved sleep quality and symptoms of depression. The benefit faded after the women stopped drinking it.\n- A trial in adults with chronic insomnia, using a chamomile extract twice daily, found no meaningful difference from placebo in how long it took to fall asleep or total sleep time.\n- Studies in older adults in care homes have reported better sleep quality scores with chamomile extract, but these studies were small and short.\n\n### Anxiety\n\nChamomile has slightly better evidence for anxiety than for sleep. In trials of people with mild to moderate generalised anxiety disorder, chamomile extract reduced anxiety symptoms more than placebo, and a longer follow-up study suggested it might reduce relapse. These trials used standardised extract capsules, not tea, so the dose was higher and more consistent than a typical cup.\n\nBecause worry is a common reason people struggle to sleep, any effect on anxiety could indirectly help with sleep.\n\n### What this means\n\nOverall, the evidence is **limited**: small studies, different products and doses, and mostly self-reported outcomes. It is reasonable to say chamomile *may* help some people feel calmer and sleep a little better. It is not reasonable to expect it to fix a serious sleep problem.\n\n## How to use chamomile for sleep\n\n- **Make it strong enough.** Use one tea bag or 1 to 2 heaped teaspoons of dried flowers per cup of just-boiled water. Cover and steep for 5 to 10 minutes; covering keeps the aromatic oils in.\n- **Drink it 30 to 60 minutes before bed**, as part of a wind-down routine.\n- **Watch the volume.** A large mug right before bed can wake you up later to go to the toilet. A small cup earlier in the evening may suit you better.\n- **Be consistent.** In the studies that showed a benefit, people took chamomile daily for at least one to four weeks.\n- **Skip the sugar or honey** close to bedtime, or brush your teeth afterwards.\n\nChamomile works best alongside good sleep habits: a regular wake-up time, a cool, dark room, less screen time before bed, and no caffeine after early afternoon.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nChamomile tea is very safe for most adults. Possible problems include:\n\n- **Allergic reactions.** Chamomile belongs to the daisy family (Asteraceae), along with ragweed, chrysanthemums and marigolds. People allergic to these plants may react to chamomile, from skin rashes to, rarely, severe allergic reactions.\n- **Drowsiness** when combined with alcohol, sleeping pills or other sedating medicines.\n- **Possible interaction with blood thinners.** There are case reports of increased bleeding risk in people taking warfarin and large amounts of chamomile. If you take an anticoagulant, check with your doctor or pharmacist before drinking chamomile regularly.\n- **Possible interaction with ciclosporin** and some other medicines broken down by the liver, based on limited evidence.\n\n> If you take a blood thinner such as warfarin, or a medicine for transplant or epilepsy, ask your pharmacist before using chamomile every day, especially as a concentrated extract.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People with a known allergy to chamomile or other daisy-family plants\n- People taking warfarin or other blood thinners, unless their doctor agrees\n- Pregnant women should stick to occasional, normal tea amounts and avoid concentrated extracts, because safety in pregnancy has not been well studied\n- Young children: occasional weak tea is generally considered fine, but check with a doctor before giving chamomile regularly to a baby or toddler\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- Poor sleep has lasted **more than three months**, or is affecting your work, mood or safety (for example, falling asleep while driving)\n- You **snore loudly**, someone has noticed you **stop breathing** during sleep, or you wake up gasping. These can be signs of sleep apnoea, which needs proper assessment.\n- You feel low, anxious or hopeless most days\n- You have restless, uncomfortable legs at night, or pain that keeps you awake\n- You are relying on alcohol or sleeping tablets to sleep\n\nFor long-term insomnia, cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment recommended by sleep experts, and it works better than herbal remedies or sleeping pills over the long run.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is chamomile tea safe to drink every night?\n\nFor most healthy adults, yes. Regular nightly use in normal tea amounts is not known to cause harm. Check with a pharmacist first if you take regular medicines.\n\n### Does chamomile tea contain caffeine?\n\nNo. Pure chamomile tea is naturally caffeine-free. Check the label of blends, which sometimes include green or black tea.\n\n### Is chamomile extract better than tea?\n\nExtracts deliver a larger, standardised dose and were used in most anxiety studies. They are also more likely to interact with medicines. For sleep, tea is a sensible place to start.\n\n### Can I give chamomile tea to my child?\n\nWeak, cooled chamomile tea is often used for children, but evidence on benefits is very limited. Do not give it to babies in place of milk, and check with your doctor for regular use.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Chamomile. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/chamomile\n- Hieu TH, et al. Therapeutic efficacy and safety of chamomile for state anxiety, generalized anxiety disorder, insomnia, and sleep quality: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *Phytotherapy Research*, 2019.\n- Chang SM, Chen CH. Effects of an intervention with drinking chamomile tea on sleep quality and depression in sleep disturbed postnatal women. *Journal of Advanced Nursing*, 2016.\n- Mao JJ, et al. Long-term chamomile therapy for generalized anxiety disorder: a randomized clinical trial. *Phytomedicine*, 2016.\n- Zick SM, et al. Preliminary examination of the efficacy and safety of a standardized chamomile extract for chronic primary insomnia. *BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine*, 2011.\n"}, {"title": "Chia Seeds: Benefits, Fibre and How to Eat Them Safely", "excerpt": "Chia seeds are a rich source of fibre and plant omega-3. Health claims beyond that are modest. Here's what they can do for digestion, and the one safety mistake to avoid.", "category": "digestive-health", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Chia seeds are a genuinely good source of fibre, which helps regularity; small trials on blood pressure, blood sugar and weight show small or inconsistent effects.", "tags": "chia seeds, fibre, constipation", "slug": "chia-seeds-benefits-fiber-and-how-to-eat-them-safely", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nChia seeds are tiny seeds from *Salvia hispanica*, a plant in the mint family. Their main, well-established benefit is **fibre**: two tablespoons provide close to 10 grams, about a third of the daily recommendation for adults. That can help with constipation and keeping bowel movements regular.\n\nThey also contain plant omega-3 (ALA), protein and minerals. Claims that chia seeds dramatically lower blood sugar, blood pressure or weight come from small studies with inconsistent results.\n\nThe main safety point: **don't eat dry chia seeds followed by a drink**. They can swell rapidly and, in rare cases, have caused blockages in the throat.\n\n## What's in chia seeds\n\nAccording to US Department of Agriculture data, about 28 grams (roughly two tablespoons) of dried chia seeds contain approximately:\n\n- **Fibre:** about 10 g, mostly insoluble, with some soluble fibre that forms a gel\n- **Protein:** about 4 to 5 g\n- **Fat:** about 9 g, of which roughly 5 g is alpha-linolenic acid (ALA), a plant omega-3\n- **Calcium, magnesium, phosphorus and iron** in useful amounts\n- **Energy:** about 140 kcal\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Digestion and regularity\n\nFibre adds bulk to stool and helps it retain water, which supports regular bowel movements. Most adults eat less fibre than recommended (around 25 to 30 g a day). Chia seeds are a convenient way to add fibre. There are few trials of chia seeds specifically for constipation, but the benefits of increasing fibre are well established.\n\n### Blood sugar\n\nChia's soluble fibre forms a gel that slows digestion. Small studies have found that adding chia seeds to a meal can reduce the rise in blood sugar afterwards. Longer trials in people with type 2 diabetes show small or inconsistent effects on overall blood sugar control.\n\n### Heart health\n\nSome small trials found modest reductions in blood pressure, especially in people with hypertension. ALA omega-3 is linked to heart health in population studies, but the body converts only a small proportion of ALA to the omega-3s found in fish (EPA and DHA). Chia is not a substitute for oily fish for that purpose.\n\n### Weight\n\nFibre and protein can increase fullness, but trials have not shown that adding chia seeds to the diet leads to meaningful weight loss on its own.\n\n## How to eat chia seeds safely\n\n1. **Soak them first.** Mix 1 tablespoon of chia seeds with about 4 tablespoons (60 ml) of liquid (water, milk or a plant drink) and leave for at least 10 to 15 minutes, or overnight to make chia pudding.\n2. **Or mix into moist foods** such as yoghurt, porridge or smoothies, and allow a few minutes before eating.\n3. **Start small.** Begin with 1 teaspoon to 1 tablespoon a day and build up to 1 to 2 tablespoons over a week or two, to avoid bloating and wind.\n4. **Drink plenty of water** through the day when you increase fibre.\n5. **Ground chia** is easier to digest and makes nutrients more accessible, but whole seeds also work.\n\n> Never eat a spoonful of dry chia seeds and then drink water. Chia seeds can absorb many times their weight in liquid. A reported case involved dry seeds swelling in the oesophagus and causing a blockage that needed hospital treatment. This risk is higher for people with swallowing difficulties.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n- **Bloating, wind and abdominal discomfort**, especially if you increase fibre quickly\n- **Constipation** if you eat a lot of fibre without enough fluid\n- **Allergy**, which is rare but has been reported, sometimes in people allergic to sesame or mustard seeds\n- **Blood pressure and diabetes medicines:** large amounts may add slightly to the effect; check your readings if you eat chia regularly\n- **Blood thinners:** omega-3s in very large amounts might affect bleeding; normal food amounts are not a concern\n- **Seeds and teeth:** small seeds can get stuck between teeth and around braces. Rinsing or flossing after eating helps.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- People with **swallowing difficulties** or narrowing of the oesophagus: only eat chia that has been fully soaked, or avoid it\n- People with **inflammatory bowel disease** during a flare, or with bowel narrowing: check with your doctor before eating high-fibre seeds\n- People with a **sesame or mustard seed allergy**\n- Young children: give soaked chia only, in small amounts, because of choking risk\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You have **constipation lasting more than three weeks** despite more fibre and fluid, or it alternates with diarrhoea\n- You notice **blood in your stool**, black stools, or unexplained weight loss\n- You have **severe abdominal pain**, a swollen abdomen or vomiting\n- You have **difficulty swallowing** or the feeling that food is stuck. Get urgent help if you cannot swallow saliva after eating chia seeds.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How many chia seeds should I eat a day?\n\nOne to two tablespoons a day is a common, sensible amount for most adults.\n\n### Do chia seeds need to be soaked?\n\nSoaking is the safest way to eat them and makes them easier to digest. Sprinkling small amounts on moist food is also fine.\n\n### Are chia seeds better than flaxseeds?\n\nBoth are good sources of fibre and ALA. Flaxseeds need grinding for the body to absorb their nutrients; chia does not. Choose the one you prefer.\n\n### Can chia seeds help with IBS?\n\nSome people with constipation-predominant IBS find soluble fibre helpful. Others find seeds make bloating worse. Introduce them slowly and see how you feel.\n\n## References\n\n- US Department of Agriculture. FoodData Central: seeds, chia seeds, dried. https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/\n- Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health. The Nutrition Source: fiber. https://nutritionsource.hsph.harvard.edu/carbohydrates/fiber/\n- Vuksan V, et al. Supplementation of conventional therapy with the novel grain Salba (Salvia hispanica L.) improves major and emerging cardiovascular risk factors in type 2 diabetes. *Diabetes Care*, 2007.\n- Rawl R, et al. Chia seed esophageal impaction (case report presented at the American College of Gastroenterology annual meeting), 2014.\n"}, {"title": "Cinnamon for Blood Sugar: Helpful or Overhyped?", "excerpt": "Cinnamon is often promoted for diabetes. Some studies show small drops in fasting blood sugar, others show nothing, and one common type of cinnamon carries a liver risk in large amounts.", "category": "heart-and-metabolism", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Meta-analyses find cinnamon may slightly lower fasting blood sugar, but results are inconsistent and it has not reliably improved HbA1c, the measure that matters most for diabetes control.", "tags": "cinnamon, blood sugar, diabetes", "slug": "cinnamon-for-blood-sugar-helpful-or-overhyped", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nCinnamon is a safe and tasty spice in normal food amounts. As a supplement for blood sugar, the evidence is **inconsistent**. Some reviews of trials found a small drop in fasting blood sugar in people with type 2 diabetes; others found no meaningful change in **HbA1c**, the long-term measure of blood sugar control that doctors use to guide treatment.\n\nCinnamon is not a treatment for diabetes. And the most common supermarket type, **cassia cinnamon**, contains coumarin, which can harm the liver if taken in large amounts every day.\n\n## Types of cinnamon\n\nThere are two main types, and the difference matters:\n\n- **Cassia cinnamon** (Chinese or Indonesian cinnamon): the most common and cheapest type in many countries. Darker, with a strong flavour. Contains relatively high levels of **coumarin**.\n- **Ceylon cinnamon** (\"true cinnamon\", from Sri Lanka): lighter, more delicate, made of thin rolled layers. Contains very little coumarin.\n\nMost studies used cassia cinnamon, often 1 to 6 grams a day.\n\n## What the research says\n\nLab and animal studies suggest that compounds in cinnamon may improve how cells respond to insulin and slow the digestion of carbohydrates. Human results are mixed:\n\n- A 2013 meta-analysis in the *Annals of Family Medicine* pooled 10 trials in people with type 2 diabetes. Cinnamon lowered fasting blood glucose by about 25 mg/dL (1.4 mmol/L) and improved cholesterol and triglycerides, but **did not significantly change HbA1c**. The studies varied widely in dose, duration and quality.\n- Other reviews have found smaller effects or no benefit, and some trials in Western populations showed no improvement at all.\n- In people with prediabetes, a small trial found a modest improvement in blood sugar response, but larger studies are needed.\n\nOverall, if cinnamon has an effect, it is small and unreliable, much smaller than diet changes, exercise, weight loss or diabetes medicines.\n\n## How people use it\n\nAs food, cinnamon is a great way to add sweetness and flavour without sugar:\n\n- Sprinkle on oats, yoghurt or fruit\n- Add to coffee or tea instead of sugar\n- Use in stews, rice and spice blends\n\nIf you are thinking about a supplement:\n\n1. **Talk to your doctor or diabetes team first**, especially if you take medicines that lower blood sugar.\n2. **Choose Ceylon cinnamon** if you plan to take cinnamon regularly in larger amounts, to limit coumarin.\n3. **Keep cassia to small amounts.** European food safety authorities set a tolerable daily intake for coumarin of 0.1 mg per kg of body weight. Cassia cinnamon can contain enough coumarin that about 1 to 2 grams a day (roughly half to one teaspoon) could reach this limit for a small adult.\n4. **Check your blood sugar** more often when you start, to look for any change and avoid lows.\n\n> Never stop or reduce diabetes medication because you are taking cinnamon. Uncontrolled blood sugar damages the eyes, kidneys, nerves and blood vessels over time, often without symptoms.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n- **Liver damage from coumarin**, with large, regular amounts of cassia cinnamon, especially in people with liver disease\n- **Low blood sugar** when combined with insulin or other glucose-lowering medicines\n- **Mouth irritation and allergic reactions**: cinnamon flavouring can cause mouth sores (cinnamon contact stomatitis), a reaction dentists recognise that is often linked to cinnamon-flavoured chewing gum\n- **Bleeding risk**: coumarin itself is not a blood thinner in these amounts, but people on warfarin should keep cinnamon intake consistent and tell their doctor about supplements\n- **Choking**: the \"cinnamon challenge\" of swallowing a spoonful of dry powder is dangerous and has caused lung injury\n\n## Who should avoid cinnamon supplements\n\n- People with **liver disease** (especially cassia cinnamon)\n- People taking **insulin or sulfonylureas**, unless their doctor agrees and they monitor blood sugar\n- People taking **medicines that can affect the liver**\n- **Pregnant or breastfeeding** women (food amounts are fine)\n- Children (food amounts are fine)\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You have symptoms of **high blood sugar**: excessive thirst, passing a lot of urine, tiredness, blurred vision, frequent infections or slow-healing wounds\n- You have **risk factors for diabetes** (family history, overweight, high blood pressure, history of gestational diabetes) and have not been tested. Type 2 diabetes is common in the Gulf region and often has no symptoms early on.\n- You have diabetes and your readings are **regularly above your target**, or you have **hypoglycaemia** (shakiness, sweating, confusion)\n- You notice **yellowing of the skin or eyes** or dark urine\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How much cinnamon should I eat a day?\n\nAs a spice in food, there is no need to measure. For regular, larger amounts, prefer Ceylon cinnamon and keep cassia to under about half a teaspoon a day.\n\n### Can cinnamon replace metformin?\n\nNo. Metformin and other diabetes medicines have strong evidence for lowering blood sugar and preventing complications. Cinnamon does not.\n\n### Does cinnamon help with weight loss?\n\nThere is no good evidence that it does.\n\n### Is cinnamon tea helpful?\n\nCinnamon tea is a pleasant, sugar-free drink. It contains much less of the active compounds than the doses studied.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Cinnamon. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/cinnamon\n- Allen RW, et al. Cinnamon use in type 2 diabetes: an updated systematic review and meta-analysis. *Annals of Family Medicine*, 2013.\n- European Food Safety Authority (EFSA). Coumarin in flavourings and other food ingredients with flavouring properties. *EFSA Journal*, 2008.\n- German Federal Institute for Risk Assessment (BfR). Frequently asked questions about coumarin in cinnamon and other foods.\n"}, {"title": "Elderberry for Colds and Flu: Benefits and Safety", "excerpt": "Elderberry syrup is a popular cold and flu remedy. A few small trials are encouraging, but evidence is limited, and raw berries, leaves and stems are toxic. Here's how to use it safely.", "category": "immunity-and-colds", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "A few small trials suggest elderberry extract may shorten cold or flu symptoms by a couple of days, but studies are small, many were industry-funded, and it should not replace flu treatment for people at risk.", "tags": "elderberry, flu, colds", "slug": "elderberry-for-colds-and-flu-benefits-and-safety", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nElderberry syrups and lozenges are widely sold to ease colds and flu. A handful of small studies found that people taking elderberry extract recovered a couple of days sooner. The evidence is **limited**: studies were small, often funded by manufacturers, and larger, independent trials are lacking.\n\nCommercial elderberry products made from cooked, ripe berries are generally safe for short-term use. **Raw or unripe berries, leaves, bark and stems** contain compounds that can release cyanide and cause nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea.\n\n## What elderberry is\n\nElderberries are the dark purple fruit of the European elder tree (*Sambucus nigra*). They are rich in **anthocyanins**, the pigments that give them their colour, along with other polyphenols. In lab studies, elderberry extracts can block some influenza viruses from entering cells and may influence immune signalling.\n\n## What the research says\n\n- **Flu:** two small early trials of a commercial elderberry syrup during flu outbreaks reported that people taking it recovered about 3 to 4 days faster than those on placebo. Both were small and funded by the manufacturer.\n- **Colds in air travellers:** a 2016 Australian trial of over 300 people flying long-haul found that elderberry extract did not significantly reduce the number of colds but appeared to shorten their duration by about two days and reduce severity.\n- **Meta-analysis:** a 2019 review pooling a few trials concluded elderberry substantially reduced upper respiratory symptoms, but it included only four small studies.\n- **A larger trial** of elderberry in adults with flu seen in emergency departments found no benefit over placebo.\n\nOverall, elderberry may help some people feel better slightly sooner, but the evidence is not strong enough to rely on, especially for flu.\n\n## How people use it\n\n- **Use commercial products** made from cooked, ripe berries, from a reputable manufacturer, and follow the dose on the label. Typical products are taken several times a day for up to 5 days, starting within 48 hours of symptoms.\n- **Homemade syrup:** if you make your own, use only fully ripe berries, remove all stems and leaves, and cook them thoroughly (simmer for at least 30 minutes). Cooking destroys the harmful compounds.\n- **Don't use it for prevention** long-term; there is little evidence for this and long-term safety is unknown.\n\nMany elderberry syrups are high in sugar. For children, and for teeth in general, take them with meals and rinse with water afterwards, or brush.\n\n> Never eat raw elderberries or use leaves, bark or stems. In one US outbreak, people became ill after drinking juice made from uncooked berries, leaves and stems.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nWhen properly prepared, elderberry is usually well tolerated for short periods. Side effects can include:\n\n- Nausea, vomiting or diarrhoea (more likely with raw or poorly prepared berries)\n- Allergic reactions in sensitive people\n\nPotential interactions (mostly theoretical, based on how elderberry acts):\n\n- **Immunosuppressant medicines** (for example, after a transplant or for autoimmune disease): elderberry may stimulate the immune system\n- **Diabetes medicines:** may slightly lower blood sugar\n- **Diuretics and laxatives:** elderberry may have mild diuretic and laxative effects\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People with **autoimmune diseases** (such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus or multiple sclerosis) or taking **immunosuppressants**, unless their doctor agrees\n- **Pregnant or breastfeeding women**, due to lack of safety data\n- **Young children**, unless using a product specifically labelled for their age and agreed with a doctor\n- People at **high risk from flu** should not use elderberry instead of antiviral medicine\n\n## Flu needs more than a home remedy for some people\n\nPeople at higher risk of flu complications should contact a doctor early, ideally within 48 hours, because antiviral medicines work best when started early. This includes:\n\n- Adults over 65 and young children, especially under 2\n- Pregnant women\n- People with asthma, COPD, heart disease, diabetes, kidney or liver disease\n- People with weakened immune systems\n\nThe annual **flu vaccine** remains the most effective way to prevent flu.\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSeek medical help if you have:\n\n- **Difficulty breathing**, shortness of breath, or chest pain\n- **Confusion**, severe drowsiness or difficulty waking\n- **Persistent high fever**, or fever that returns after improving\n- **Severe weakness**, muscle pain or not passing urine\n- Symptoms that **improve and then get worse**, which can signal a secondary infection such as pneumonia\n- A child who is breathing fast, has blue lips, is not drinking, or has a rash with fever\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does elderberry boost the immune system?\n\nLab studies show elderberry can influence immune signals, but that does not prove it improves immune protection in people. Good sleep, nutrition and vaccination are proven ways to support immunity.\n\n### Is elderberry safe for children?\n\nSome products are made for children, but evidence on effectiveness and safety in children is very limited. Check with your doctor or pharmacist and never give homemade preparations to children.\n\n### Can I take elderberry with Tamiflu?\n\nThere is no known interaction, but check with your doctor or pharmacist. Elderberry should never replace prescribed antiviral treatment.\n\n### How long can I take elderberry?\n\nStudies used it for up to about 5 days during an illness. Long-term safety is unknown.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). European elder. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/european-elder\n- Tiralongo E, Wee SS, Lea RA. Elderberry supplementation reduces cold duration and symptoms in air-travellers: a randomized, double-blind placebo-controlled clinical trial. *Nutrients*, 2016.\n- Hawkins J, et al. Black elderberry supplementation effectively treats upper respiratory symptoms: a meta-analysis of randomized, controlled clinical trials. *Complementary Therapies in Medicine*, 2019.\n- US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Poisoning from elderberry juice, California, 1983. *Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report*, 1984.\n"}, {"title": "Garlic for Heart Health: Benefits, Risks and Medicine Interactions", "excerpt": "Garlic supplements can lower blood pressure modestly in people with hypertension. Here's how big the effect is, which products were studied, and why garlic and blood thinners don't mix.", "category": "heart-and-metabolism", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Meta-analyses show garlic supplements, especially aged garlic extract, lower blood pressure modestly in people with high blood pressure; effects on cholesterol are small and inconsistent.", "tags": "garlic, blood pressure, cholesterol", "slug": "garlic-for-heart-health-benefits-risks-and-medication-intera", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nGarlic is one of the better-studied heart-health supplements. Pooled results from randomised trials show that garlic supplements lower blood pressure by an average of around **8 to 9 mmHg systolic and 6 mmHg diastolic** in people with high blood pressure, similar to a single standard blood pressure medicine at a low dose. Effects on cholesterol are small and inconsistent.\n\nGarlic can add to the effect of **blood thinners** and raise bleeding risk, and it can reduce the effectiveness of some medicines. Garlic in food is safe and healthy; supplements need more care.\n\n## How garlic might work\n\nWhen garlic is crushed or chopped, a compound called **alliin** is converted into **allicin**, which breaks down into a range of sulphur compounds. These may:\n\n- Help blood vessels relax, partly by increasing nitric oxide and hydrogen sulphide production\n- Slightly reduce cholesterol production in the liver\n- Make blood platelets less sticky, which is why garlic affects bleeding\n\nAged garlic extract, made by soaking garlic for many months, contains a stable compound called **S-allylcysteine**, which is used in many trials.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Blood pressure\n\nA 2016 meta-analysis in the *Journal of Nutrition* combined 20 trials and found that garlic supplements reduced systolic blood pressure by about 8.6 mmHg and diastolic by about 6.1 mmHg in people with hypertension. There was little effect in people with normal blood pressure. Aged garlic extract was the most commonly studied product, and trials usually lasted 8 to 12 weeks.\n\nA reduction of this size, if sustained, is meaningful: lowering systolic blood pressure by around 10 mmHg is linked to a substantially lower risk of heart attack and stroke. However, garlic trials were smaller and shorter than drug trials, and they did not measure heart attacks or strokes directly.\n\n### Cholesterol\n\nReviews suggest garlic may lower total and LDL cholesterol slightly, by around 5 to 10% in some analyses, with more effect in people whose levels are high and in longer trials. Results vary a lot between products and studies.\n\n### Other claims\n\nGarlic has been studied for preventing colds and certain cancers, with weak or inconsistent results.\n\n## How people use it\n\n**In food:** Garlic is part of a healthy Mediterranean or Middle Eastern style diet. Crushing or chopping garlic and leaving it for about 10 minutes before cooking allows more allicin to form. Food amounts are safe for nearly everyone.\n\n**As a supplement:** trial doses typically included:\n\n- **Aged garlic extract:** around 600 to 2,400 mg a day\n- **Garlic powder tablets:** around 600 to 900 mg a day\n\nIf you want to try a supplement for blood pressure:\n\n1. **Talk to your doctor first**, especially if you already take blood pressure medicine, as the effects can add up.\n2. **Check your blood pressure** at home or at a pharmacy before starting and after 8 to 12 weeks.\n3. **Keep taking any prescribed medicines.** Garlic may be an addition, not a replacement.\n4. **Stop garlic supplements 7 to 10 days before surgery or dental extractions**, and tell your surgeon or dentist.\n\n> Tell your dentist or surgeon if you take garlic supplements, fish oil, ginkgo or similar products. Together with blood thinners they can increase bleeding after procedures such as tooth extractions.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nCommon side effects:\n\n- **Bad breath and body odour**, which are less noticeable with aged garlic extract\n- **Heartburn, wind and stomach upset**\n- **Skin irritation or burns** if raw garlic is applied to skin\n\nInteractions:\n\n- **Blood thinners and antiplatelet medicines** (such as warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, clopidogrel, aspirin): increased bleeding risk\n- **Saquinavir and some other HIV medicines**: garlic can lower their blood levels and make them less effective\n- **Blood pressure medicines**: added effect, possibly causing dizziness\n- **Some medicines processed by the liver**, based on limited evidence\n\n## Who should avoid garlic supplements\n\n- People taking **blood thinners or antiplatelet medicines**, unless their doctor agrees\n- People with **bleeding disorders**\n- People with **surgery or dental procedures** in the next one to two weeks\n- People taking **HIV medicines**\n- Pregnant or breastfeeding women (food amounts are fine)\n- People with **stomach ulcers or bad reflux** may find garlic irritating\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nHigh blood pressure usually has **no symptoms**, so regular checks are important. See a doctor if:\n\n- Your home readings are **regularly 135/85 mmHg or higher**, or a clinic reading is 140/90 or higher\n- You have a reading of **180/120 or higher**, especially with headache, chest pain, shortness of breath, vision changes or confusion. Seek urgent care.\n- You have unusual **bruising, nosebleeds or bleeding gums** while taking garlic supplements\n- You have chest pain, breathlessness on exertion, or palpitations\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is raw garlic better than supplements?\n\nRaw garlic forms allicin when crushed, but the amount varies and many people can't eat enough comfortably. Most trials used standardised supplements. Both can be part of a healthy diet.\n\n### How long does garlic take to lower blood pressure?\n\nIn trials, effects appeared over 8 to 12 weeks of daily use.\n\n### Can garlic replace my blood pressure medicine?\n\nNo. Never stop prescribed medicine without talking to your doctor.\n\n### Does garlic help bleeding gums?\n\nNo, and it may make bleeding slightly worse in people on blood thinners. Bleeding gums are usually a sign of gum disease and need a dental check.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Garlic. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/garlic\n- Ried K. Garlic lowers blood pressure in hypertensive individuals, regulates serum cholesterol, and stimulates immunity: an updated meta-analysis and review. *Journal of Nutrition*, 2016.\n- Ried K, Toben C, Fakler P. Effect of garlic on serum lipids: an updated meta-analysis. *Nutrition Reviews*, 2013.\n- Ettehad D, et al. Blood pressure lowering for prevention of cardiovascular disease and death: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *The Lancet*, 2016.\n"}, {"title": "Ginger for Nausea: Benefits, How to Use It and Side Effects", "excerpt": "Ginger is one of the better-studied natural remedies, especially for morning sickness. Here is what it can and can't do, how much to take, and when nausea needs a doctor.", "category": "digestive-health", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Several trials and reviews show ginger can ease nausea in early pregnancy, and obstetric guidelines list it as an option; results for chemotherapy, surgery and motion sickness are mixed.", "tags": "ginger, nausea, pregnancy", "slug": "ginger-for-nausea-benefits-uses-and-side-effects", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nGinger can reduce mild to moderate nausea for many people, and the best evidence is for **nausea and vomiting in early pregnancy**. The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists lists ginger as a reasonable non-drug option for morning sickness. Results for nausea after surgery, during chemotherapy and for motion sickness are mixed: it may help some people a little, but it is not a reliable replacement for anti-sickness medicines.\n\nGinger is generally safe in food amounts and modest supplement doses. Higher doses can cause heartburn, and people on blood thinners should check with a pharmacist first.\n\n## How ginger might work\n\nGinger root contains compounds called **gingerols** and **shogaols**. They appear to act on the digestive tract and on nerve signals involved in nausea, including blocking some serotonin receptors (the same target as some anti-sickness medicines, though much more weakly) and helping the stomach empty more steadily.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Pregnancy nausea\n\nNausea affects most pregnant women in the first trimester. Several randomised trials and meta-analyses have found that ginger reduces nausea more than placebo, and in some trials about as much as vitamin B6. A Cochrane review of treatments for nausea and vomiting in early pregnancy found the evidence for most remedies, including ginger, to be of low quality, but ginger was among the more promising options. Ginger seems to help nausea more than vomiting.\n\n### Chemotherapy\n\nSome trials found that adding ginger to standard anti-sickness medicine reduced nausea on the day of chemotherapy; others found no benefit. Ginger should only ever be added to, never used instead of, the medicines prescribed by the cancer team, and it should be discussed with them first because of possible effects on bleeding and drug interactions.\n\n### Surgery and motion sickness\n\nResults after surgery are inconsistent. For motion sickness, small, older studies give mixed results. Ginger is unlikely to do harm if you want to try it for travel, but standard motion-sickness medicines are more reliable for people badly affected.\n\n## How to use ginger for nausea\n\nStudies of pregnancy nausea typically used about **1 gram of ginger a day**, split into several smaller doses. As a rough guide, 1 gram of dried ginger is about half a teaspoon of ground ginger, or roughly 4 grams (a thumb-sized piece, about 2 to 3 cm) of fresh root.\n\nWays to take it:\n\n- **Ginger tea:** slice 2 to 3 cm of fresh ginger, pour over a cup of boiling water and steep for 5 to 10 minutes. Sip slowly through the day.\n- **Crystallised ginger or ginger biscuits:** convenient, but high in sugar.\n- **Capsules:** usually 250 mg taken up to four times a day in pregnancy studies. Choose a reputable brand.\n- **Ginger ale and soft drinks:** most contain very little real ginger, so they are unlikely to help beyond the sugar and fluid.\n\nOther things that help nausea alongside ginger:\n\n1. Eat small amounts often, and do not let your stomach get completely empty.\n2. Try plain, dry foods first thing in the morning, such as crackers or toast.\n3. Sip fluids between meals rather than with them.\n4. Avoid strong smells and greasy or spicy foods that trigger nausea.\n5. Get fresh air and rest when you can.\n\n> Frequent vomiting is acidic and can wear down tooth enamel. After vomiting, rinse your mouth with water (or water with a little baking soda) rather than brushing straight away, and wait about an hour before brushing.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nGinger is generally well tolerated. Side effects are usually mild:\n\n- Heartburn, indigestion or burping\n- Stomach discomfort or diarrhoea, especially at higher doses\n- Mouth or throat irritation from strong fresh ginger\n\nPotential interactions:\n\n- **Blood thinners and antiplatelet medicines.** Ginger may have a mild effect on blood clotting. The evidence is mixed, but people taking warfarin, apixaban, clopidogrel or similar medicines should check with a pharmacist before taking ginger supplements.\n- **Diabetes medicines.** Large amounts may lower blood sugar slightly.\n- **Blood pressure medicines.** Ginger may lower blood pressure slightly.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- **Pregnancy:** ginger in the doses above appears safe based on available studies, but check with your midwife or doctor before taking supplements, and avoid high doses near the time of delivery because of the theoretical bleeding risk.\n- People with **gallstones**, since ginger may increase bile flow\n- People taking **blood thinners**, as above\n- Anyone with **reflux**, if ginger makes heartburn worse\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nGinger is for mild nausea. Get medical help if:\n\n- You **cannot keep fluids down for 24 hours**, or are vomiting repeatedly\n- You have signs of **dehydration**: very dark urine or not passing urine for 8 hours, dizziness when standing, a dry mouth and lips\n- You are pregnant and losing weight, or nausea is affecting your daily life. Severe pregnancy sickness (hyperemesis gravidarum) needs treatment and is not something to manage with home remedies alone.\n- There is **blood or \"coffee grounds\"** in the vomit, or black stools\n- You have **severe abdominal pain**, a stiff neck, severe headache, chest pain or confusion with the nausea\n- Nausea has lasted more than a few days without a clear cause\n- A baby or young child is vomiting and not feeding, unusually sleepy, or has fewer wet nappies\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How quickly does ginger work for nausea?\n\nSome people notice an effect within 30 to 60 minutes. In pregnancy trials, improvement was measured over several days of regular use.\n\n### Is ginger safe in pregnancy?\n\nStudies of ginger at about 1 gram a day in early pregnancy have not shown harm to the baby, and it is included in obstetric guidance as an option. Always tell your midwife or doctor about any supplements.\n\n### Can children take ginger for nausea?\n\nSmall amounts of ginger in food or weak tea are generally fine for older children. Evidence for children is limited, so talk to a doctor before using supplements.\n\n### Is fresh ginger better than powder?\n\nBoth contain active compounds; drying changes some gingerols into shogaols. Either form can be used. Capsules make the dose more consistent.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Ginger. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ginger\n- American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists. Practice Bulletin No. 189: Nausea and Vomiting of Pregnancy. *Obstetrics & Gynecology*, 2018.\n- Matthews A, et al. Interventions for nausea and vomiting in early pregnancy. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*, 2015.\n- Viljoen E, et al. A systematic review and meta-analysis of the effect and safety of ginger in the treatment of pregnancy-associated nausea and vomiting. *Nutrition Journal*, 2014.\n"}, {"title": "Green Tea Benefits: Energy, Antioxidants and Who Should Limit It", "excerpt": "Green tea is a healthy everyday drink with modest benefits. Concentrated extracts are a different story. Here's what the evidence shows and who should be careful.", "category": "heart-and-metabolism", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Drinking green tea is linked with lower heart disease risk in population studies and may slightly lower LDL cholesterol, but weight-loss effects are tiny and high-dose extracts can harm the liver.", "tags": "green tea, caffeine, cholesterol", "slug": "green-tea-benefits-energy-antioxidants-and-who-should-limit-", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nBrewed green tea is a healthy drink for most people. Population studies, especially in Japan and China, link regular green tea drinking with a lower risk of heart disease and stroke, and trials suggest it may **slightly lower LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol**. Its gentle caffeine content can improve alertness.\n\nThe popular claims for **weight loss** are mostly overstated: any effect is very small. **Green tea extract supplements** are not the same as tea. High doses have caused liver injury, and green tea can interact with some medicines.\n\n## What is in green tea\n\nGreen tea comes from the same plant as black tea, *Camellia sinensis*, but the leaves are heated soon after picking to stop them oxidising. This keeps high levels of **catechins**, a group of polyphenols, with the best known being **EGCG** (epigallocatechin gallate). Green tea also contains:\n\n- **Caffeine:** about 20 to 45 mg per cup (roughly a third to half of a cup of coffee), depending on the tea and how it is brewed\n- **L-theanine:** an amino acid that may promote calm alertness, especially combined with caffeine\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Heart and blood vessels\n\nLarge observational studies following hundreds of thousands of people have found that regular green tea drinkers have a lower risk of cardiovascular disease and death. A Japanese study of over 40,000 adults found lower cardiovascular death rates in people drinking five or more cups a day. Observational studies cannot prove cause and effect, because green tea drinkers may differ in other healthy ways, but the findings are consistent.\n\nRandomised trials and meta-analyses show green tea or catechins **lower LDL cholesterol modestly**, on the order of a few percent, and may lower blood pressure slightly.\n\n### Weight\n\nGreen tea catechins and caffeine may slightly increase energy use. Reviews, including a Cochrane review, found that green tea preparations produced only a **very small, not clinically important weight loss** in overweight adults, often less than 1 kg, and studies varied a lot.\n\n### Alertness and mood\n\nCaffeine improves alertness and reaction time. Small studies suggest the combination of caffeine and L-theanine may improve attention more smoothly than caffeine alone.\n\n### Cancer and other claims\n\nLab studies are interesting, but human studies have not shown that green tea prevents cancer. The evidence is inconsistent, and it should never be used as a cancer treatment.\n\n## How to enjoy green tea\n\n- **Brew it well:** use water just below boiling (about 70 to 80 °C) and steep for 2 to 3 minutes. Boiling water and long steeping make it bitter.\n- **Two to three cups a day** is a sensible amount for most adults and gives a moderate amount of caffeine. Healthy adults can usually have up to about 400 mg of caffeine a day from all sources.\n- **Avoid late-afternoon cups** if caffeine affects your sleep.\n- **Drink it between meals** if you are prone to low iron, because tea reduces the absorption of iron from plant foods.\n- **Matcha** is powdered whole tea leaf, so it contains more catechins and caffeine per serving.\n\n> Green tea extract capsules and \"fat burner\" supplements can contain the catechins of 10 or more cups of tea in a single dose. European food safety experts concluded that 800 mg or more of EGCG a day from supplements may raise liver enzymes. Avoid high-dose extracts, especially on an empty stomach.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n**Brewed tea** side effects are mostly from caffeine: trouble sleeping, jitteriness, a fast heartbeat, headaches, or acid reflux in some people.\n\n**Extracts** have been linked to rare but serious **liver injury**, sometimes needing transplantation. Risk seems higher with high doses and fasting.\n\nInteractions:\n\n- **Nadolol** (a beta-blocker): green tea can greatly reduce its absorption\n- **Warfarin:** large amounts of green tea may contain enough vitamin K to affect warfarin; keep your intake consistent\n- **Stimulant medicines** and other caffeine sources add up\n- **Iron:** reduces absorption of non-haem iron from food\n- **Some antidepressants and medicines** processed by the liver may be affected by high-dose extracts\n\n## Who should limit it\n\n- **Pregnant women:** limit total caffeine to 200 mg a day (about four cups of green tea, counting all other caffeine sources too) and avoid extracts\n- People with **iron-deficiency anaemia**: drink between meals rather than with them\n- People with **anxiety, palpitations or insomnia** that caffeine worsens\n- People with **liver disease**: avoid extracts entirely\n- Anyone taking **nadolol** or **warfarin**: talk to your doctor or pharmacist\n- Children: limit caffeine and avoid extracts\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\n- You notice signs of **liver problems** after taking a supplement: yellow skin or eyes, dark urine, pale stools, abdominal pain or unusual tiredness. Stop the supplement and see a doctor promptly.\n- You have **palpitations**, chest pain or feel faint\n- You are managing **high cholesterol, high blood pressure or heart disease**. Tea can be part of a healthy diet, but it does not replace treatment.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does green tea help you lose weight?\n\nOnly very slightly, if at all. It can be a healthy, unsweetened replacement for sugary drinks, which is where most of its weight benefit is likely to come from.\n\n### Is decaffeinated green tea still healthy?\n\nDecaf green tea still contains catechins, although some are lost during processing. It is a good choice if caffeine bothers you.\n\n### Does green tea stain teeth?\n\nLess than black tea or coffee, but it can contribute to staining. Rinsing with water afterwards helps. Unsweetened tea is far kinder to teeth than sugary drinks.\n\n### How many cups is too many?\n\nFor most adults, the limit is set by caffeine: up to about eight cups spread over the day is usually within safe caffeine limits, but most people feel best with fewer.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Green tea. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea\n- Kuriyama S, et al. Green tea consumption and mortality due to cardiovascular disease, cancer, and all causes in Japan: the Ohsaki study. *JAMA*, 2006.\n- Jurgens TM, et al. Green tea for weight loss and weight maintenance in overweight or obese adults. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*, 2012.\n- European Food Safety Authority (EFSA). Scientific opinion on the safety of green tea catechins. *EFSA Journal*, 2018.\n"}, {"title": "Honey for Cough: What the Evidence Shows and How to Use It Safely", "excerpt": "Honey is one of the few home cough remedies with decent evidence behind it. Here is how much to take, who should avoid it, and when a cough needs a doctor.", "category": "immunity-and-colds", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Reviews of trials in children and adults find honey eases cough symptoms a little better than no treatment, and about as well as some over-the-counter cough medicines.", "tags": "honey, cough, colds", "slug": "honey-for-cough-science-backed-benefits-and-safety-guideline", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nA spoonful of honey can make a cough from a cold less frequent and less disruptive, especially at night. It is one of the few home remedies recommended in official guidance: the UK's NICE guideline on acute cough suggests honey as a reasonable first step for adults and children over one year old.\n\nHoney does not cure the infection or shorten how long the cold lasts. It is a way to feel more comfortable while your body clears the virus. And it must **never** be given to babies under 12 months old.\n\n## Why honey might help\n\nNobody is completely sure how honey works on a cough, but a few explanations are likely to play a part:\n\n- **Coating effect.** Honey is thick and sticky. It coats the back of the throat, which may calm the irritated nerve endings that trigger the cough reflex.\n- **Sweet taste.** Sweet tastes appear to dampen the cough reflex through shared nerve pathways, which may be part of why syrups of all kinds feel soothing.\n- **More saliva.** Swallowing honey increases saliva and swallowing, which can reduce the tickle that sets off coughing.\n- **Antimicrobial and anti-inflammatory compounds.** Honey contains substances that slow bacterial growth in the lab. This is interesting, but colds are caused by viruses, so it probably matters less for a typical cough.\n\n## What the research says\n\nThe best summary of the evidence in children is a Cochrane review, which pooled randomised trials of honey for acute cough. It found that honey probably relieves cough symptoms better than no treatment, a placebo or the antihistamine diphenhydramine, and may work about as well as dextromethorphan, a common cough-suppressing ingredient. Parents in these studies also reported better sleep, both for the child and for themselves.\n\nFor adults and children together, a 2021 systematic review in *BMJ Evidence-Based Medicine* looked at honey for upper respiratory tract infections. Honey improved overall symptom scores and cough frequency and severity compared with usual care. The authors noted that most studies were small and some were at risk of bias, so the size of the benefit is uncertain, but the direction was consistent.\n\nPut simply, the evidence is **moderate**: good enough that health authorities recommend honey for self-care, but not strong enough to say exactly how much it helps or that it beats medicines.\n\n## How to use honey for a cough\n\nThere is no single proven dose. The amounts used in trials and suggested by health services are roughly:\n\n- **Children aged 1 to 5:** half a teaspoon (about 2.5 ml)\n- **Children aged 6 to 11:** one teaspoon (about 5 ml)\n- **Age 12 and over, including adults:** two teaspoons (about 10 ml)\n\nPractical tips:\n\n1. **Take it before bed.** Night-time coughing is where honey seems to help most. Give it up to 30 minutes before sleep.\n2. **Take it straight or in a warm drink.** A spoon on its own gives the best coating. You can also stir it into warm water with lemon, or into warm (not boiling) herbal tea.\n3. **Repeat as needed during the day**, but keep an eye on the total amount of sugar, especially for children.\n4. **Brush teeth afterwards.** This is the step dentists would underline. Honey is mostly sugar and sticks to teeth. A bedtime dose followed by sleep without brushing is a recipe for tooth decay, particularly in children. If brushing after the dose is not practical, at least have the child drink some water.\n\nAny type of honey seems to work. Some studies used dark honeys such as buckwheat, and manuka honey is often marketed for its antibacterial properties, but there is no good evidence that expensive honeys work better for a cough than ordinary honey.\n\n> **Never give honey to a baby under 12 months old.** Honey can contain spores of the bacteria that cause infant botulism, a rare but serious illness. This applies to raw, pasteurised and cooked honey, and to foods made with it.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nFor most people over one year old, honey is very safe in the amounts above. Things to keep in mind:\n\n- **Tooth decay**, as described above.\n- **Blood sugar.** People with diabetes can usually use a small amount, but it counts as sugar. Check your levels as usual and talk to your diabetes team if you are unsure.\n- **Allergy.** Rarely, people react to pollen or bee proteins in honey. Avoid it if you have had a reaction to honey or bee products before.\n- **Calories.** Several spoonfuls a day for a week adds up, which matters for people managing their weight.\n\nHoney has no important interactions with medicines in normal food amounts.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- Babies under 12 months old (never give honey)\n- Anyone with a known allergy to honey, pollen or bee products\n- People with diabetes should use it cautiously and count it in their carbohydrate intake\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nMost coughs from a cold settle within three weeks. Honey is for that kind of cough. See a doctor or seek urgent care if:\n\n- The cough lasts **more than three weeks**\n- You or your child is **struggling to breathe**, breathing very fast, or the skin between the ribs pulls in with each breath\n- There is **chest pain**, or you are **coughing up blood**\n- There is a **high fever**, or a fever in a baby under three months old\n- The cough comes in **bouts with a \"whoop\"**, vomiting, or turning red or blue, which can be a sign of whooping cough\n- You have **lost weight** without trying, have night sweats, or have a long-term lung or heart condition and your symptoms are getting worse\n- A child seems unusually drowsy, floppy or is not drinking\n\nGet emergency help straight away for severe breathing difficulty, blue lips or confusion.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is honey better than cough syrup?\n\nFor children, studies suggest honey works at least as well as some common over-the-counter cough ingredients. Many cough medicines are not recommended for young children anyway, so honey is a sensible first choice for children over one year old.\n\n### Can I mix honey with lemon or ginger?\n\nYes. Warm water with honey and lemon is a comforting drink and adds fluid, which helps when you are unwell. Just keep the water warm rather than boiling.\n\n### Does honey work for a dry cough or a chesty cough?\n\nStudies mostly included coughs caused by colds and other short-term infections, which can be dry or chesty. Honey eases the urge to cough rather than treating the cause, so it can help with both.\n\n### How long can I keep using honey for a cough?\n\nIt is fine to use for the length of a normal cold. If you still need it after three weeks, the cough itself needs checking by a doctor.\n\n## References\n\n- National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE). Cough (acute): antimicrobial prescribing, guideline NG120, 2019. https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng120\n- Oduwole O, Udoh EE, Oyo-Ita A, Meremikwu MM. Honey for acute cough in children. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*, 2018.\n- Abuelgasim H, Albury C, Lee J. Effectiveness of honey for symptomatic relief in upper respiratory tract infections: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *BMJ Evidence-Based Medicine*, 2021.\n- Paul IM, et al. Effect of honey, dextromethorphan, and no treatment on nocturnal cough and sleep quality for coughing children and their parents. *Archives of Pediatrics & Adolescent Medicine*, 2007.\n- US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Botulism: infant botulism. https://www.cdc.gov/botulism/\n"}, {"title": "Lavender for Sleep and Relaxation: Benefits and Safety", "excerpt": "Lavender oil is used for calm and better sleep. A specific oral lavender oil has decent evidence for anxiety, while evidence for sleep from scents and sprays is limited. Here's how to use it safely.", "category": "sleep-and-stress", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Small studies suggest lavender aromatherapy may slightly improve self-reported sleep quality; a standardised oral lavender oil capsule has better evidence for anxiety, but sleep evidence remains limited.", "tags": "lavender, aromatherapy, sleep", "slug": "lavender-for-sleep-and-relaxation-benefits-and-safety", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nLavender has a long reputation as a calming herb. The evidence splits into two areas:\n\n- **Lavender aromatherapy** (inhaling the scent) has been tested in small studies, often in hospital or care settings. Some found modest improvements in self-reported sleep quality and relaxation, but studies are small and hard to blind, since people know when they are smelling lavender.\n- **Oral lavender oil capsules**, particularly a standardised preparation called **Silexan**, have been tested in larger trials for anxiety and showed benefits similar to some prescription anti-anxiety medicines in a few studies. Some of these trials also reported better sleep as a secondary result.\n\nLavender is low-risk when inhaled. Swallowing essential oil from a bottle is dangerous, and capsules should be used only as directed.\n\n## How lavender might work\n\nLavender oil contains **linalool** and **linalyl acetate**. In animal studies these compounds have calming effects and may act on GABA receptors and calcium channels in the nervous system, similar in some ways to how certain calming medicines work but much milder. The scent may also act through the brain's emotional and memory centres; a familiar, pleasant smell can be relaxing in itself.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Aromatherapy and sleep\n\nSystematic reviews of lavender inhalation report small improvements in sleep quality in some groups, such as students, older adults and patients in intensive care or heart units. Common problems with these studies include small sizes, short duration, and no good placebo, because a convincing fake lavender scent is hard to make.\n\n### Oral lavender oil and anxiety\n\nThe best-studied product is Silexan, an 80 mg capsule of standardised lavender oil sold in several European countries. Several randomised trials in people with generalised anxiety disorder or anxiety-related restlessness found it reduced anxiety scores more than placebo over 6 to 10 weeks, and two trials found results similar to the prescription medicines lorazepam and paroxetine. Many participants also reported better sleep. Most of these trials were funded by the manufacturer, and independent replication is limited.\n\n### Overall\n\nFor sleep, the evidence is **limited**: lavender may help some people feel calmer and sleep a little better, but it is not a proven treatment for insomnia.\n\n## How to use lavender\n\n**For aromatherapy:**\n\n1. Add 3 to 5 drops of lavender essential oil to a **diffuser** in the bedroom for 30 minutes before bed, in a ventilated room.\n2. Or put 1 to 2 drops on a cotton pad or tissue near your pillow (not directly on the pillow if you have sensitive skin).\n3. For skin use, always **dilute**: about 1 to 2 drops of essential oil in a teaspoon (5 ml) of a carrier oil such as almond or sunflower oil. Do a patch test first.\n4. Lavender tea (from dried flowers) and bath products are pleasant ways to relax, though evidence for them is minimal.\n\n**Oral capsules** should only be standardised products from a pharmacy, taken at the dose on the label (usually 80 mg once daily). Talk to a pharmacist or doctor before starting, especially if you take other medicines.\n\n> Never swallow lavender essential oil from a bottle, and keep essential oils out of reach of children. Even small amounts swallowed by a child can cause drowsiness, vomiting or breathing problems. If a child swallows essential oil, contact a poison centre or emergency services.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n**Inhaled or on the skin:**\n\n- Skin irritation or allergic contact dermatitis, especially with undiluted oil\n- Headache or nausea in people sensitive to strong scents\n- Essential oils diffused around pets, especially cats, can be harmful to them\n\n**Oral capsules:**\n\n- Burping with a lavender taste, indigestion and nausea are the most common side effects\n- Possible added drowsiness with alcohol, sedatives, sleeping pills or anti-anxiety medicines\n\n**Hormonal effects:** a small number of case reports have described breast development in young boys who were regularly exposed to products containing lavender and tea tree oil, which resolved after stopping. Lab studies suggest these oils may weakly affect hormone signalling. The link is not proven, but it is sensible to avoid regular use of these oils on children's skin.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- **Children:** avoid swallowing any essential oil, and avoid regular skin application of lavender or tea tree products in young children\n- **Pregnant or breastfeeding women:** aromatherapy in moderation is generally considered low risk; avoid oral capsules unless advised by a doctor\n- People taking **sedatives, sleeping tablets or anti-anxiety medicines**: oral lavender may add to their effects\n- People with **asthma** who find strong scents trigger symptoms\n- Anyone with **sensitive skin or eczema**: dilute well and patch test\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You have had **trouble sleeping for more than three months**, or it is affecting your daytime life\n- You **snore loudly**, wake up choking, or feel very sleepy during the day, which can be signs of sleep apnoea\n- **Anxiety or worry** is present most days, causing physical symptoms like a racing heart, or stopping you from doing things\n- You feel low, hopeless, or have thoughts of harming yourself. Seek help urgently.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does lavender spray on the pillow work?\n\nIt may help you feel relaxed as part of a bedtime routine. Strong evidence that it improves sleep is lacking, but it is low-risk if you don't have skin sensitivity.\n\n### Is lavender oil safe for babies?\n\nAvoid putting essential oils on babies' skin or diffusing them in a baby's room, as babies are more sensitive to them. Talk to your doctor first.\n\n### Can lavender replace sleeping pills?\n\nNo, not for diagnosed insomnia or anxiety. Talk to your doctor before stopping any prescribed medicine.\n\n### What is the difference between lavender oil and lavender essential oil?\n\nEssential oil is highly concentrated. \"Lavender oil\" in cosmetics is often diluted. Oral capsules use a specific standardised, food-grade preparation.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Lavender. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/lavender\n- Kasper S, et al. Lavender oil preparation Silexan is effective in generalized anxiety disorder: a randomized, double-blind comparison to placebo and paroxetine. *International Journal of Neuropsychopharmacology*, 2014.\n- Henley DV, et al. Prepubertal gynecomastia linked to lavender and tea tree oils. *New England Journal of Medicine*, 2007.\n- Lillehei AS, Halcon LL. A systematic review of the effect of inhaled essential oils on sleep. *Journal of Alternative and Complementary Medicine*, 2014.\n"}, {"title": "Lemon Water in the Morning: Benefits, Myths and Tooth Safety", "excerpt": "Lemon water is a pleasant way to drink more water, but it doesn't detox your body or melt fat. It can, however, wear down tooth enamel. Here's how to enjoy it safely.", "category": "everyday-wellness", "evidence_level": "weak", "evidence_summary": "The real benefit of lemon water is helping people drink more fluid; claims about detox, weight loss and \"alkalising\" the body are not supported by evidence.", "tags": "lemon water, hydration, teeth", "slug": "lemon-water-in-the-morning-benefits-myths-and-tooth-safety", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nLemon water is simply water with lemon juice. Its main benefit is that it makes water more appealing, which helps some people drink more. It provides a little vitamin C, and the citrate in lemon juice may help some people prone to certain kidney stones.\n\nThe popular claims that lemon water **detoxifies the body, burns fat, boosts metabolism or alkalises the body** are not supported by evidence. And because lemon juice is very acidic, drinking it regularly, especially by sipping slowly through the morning, can **erode tooth enamel**. This is the risk most articles leave out.\n\n## The claims, checked\n\n### \"It detoxifies your body\"\n\nYour liver and kidneys remove waste products continuously. There is no evidence that lemon water helps them work better. Drinking enough water does support the kidneys, but plain water does that just as well.\n\n### \"It helps you lose weight\"\n\nThere is no good evidence that lemon water burns fat or meaningfully boosts metabolism. Drinking water before meals may help some people eat slightly less, and replacing sugary drinks with lemon water cuts calories. Those are real benefits, but they come from the water and the drinks you are not having, not the lemon.\n\n### \"It alkalises your body\"\n\nLemon juice is acidic. Some argue it has an \"alkalising effect\" after digestion. Whatever happens in your urine, your blood pH is kept within a very narrow range by your lungs and kidneys, and food does not change it in healthy people.\n\n### \"It boosts immunity\"\n\nThe juice of half a lemon contains about 10 to 15 mg of vitamin C, a modest amount compared with the daily need of around 75 to 90 mg for adults. It contributes, but it won't protect you from colds on its own.\n\n### What might genuinely help\n\n- **Hydration:** if lemon makes you drink more water, that is good, especially in hot climates.\n- **Kidney stones:** citrate in lemon juice can help prevent calcium kidney stones in some people by binding calcium in the urine. Some kidney specialists suggest lemon juice in water as part of prevention, though medicines (potassium citrate) have stronger evidence. Discuss this with your doctor if you have had kidney stones.\n- **Less sugar:** using lemon to flavour water instead of drinking juice or soft drinks.\n\n## How lemon water affects your teeth\n\nLemon juice has a pH of around 2 to 3, similar to many soft drinks. Acid softens the outer surface of tooth enamel, and repeated exposure gradually wears it away. This is called **dental erosion**, and lost enamel does not grow back. Signs include:\n\n- Sensitivity to hot, cold or sweet foods\n- Teeth looking more yellow (as the darker layer underneath shows through)\n- Edges of the front teeth looking thin or translucent\n- Fillings that seem to stand out\n\nThe biggest risk comes from **sipping slowly over a long time**, such as nursing a bottle of lemon water through the morning, because your teeth are exposed to acid repeatedly.\n\n## How to drink lemon water more safely\n\n1. **Dilute it well.** Use the juice of a quarter to half a lemon in a large glass (250 to 300 ml) of water.\n2. **Drink it in one go** or with a meal, rather than sipping for hours.\n3. **Use a straw** to reduce contact with your teeth.\n4. **Rinse your mouth with plain water** afterwards.\n5. **Don't brush straight away.** Brushing softened enamel can wear it further. Wait at least 30 to 60 minutes.\n6. **Use fluoride toothpaste** twice a day, which helps strengthen enamel.\n7. **Avoid sucking lemon slices** or eating lemons whole.\n8. **Avoid drinking it right before bed**, when saliva flow drops and acid stays in contact with teeth for longer.\n\n> If you already have sensitive teeth or visible enamel wear, plain water is the better daily choice. Ask your dentist about protecting your enamel.\n\n## Other side effects\n\n- **Heartburn:** acidic drinks can trigger reflux symptoms in some people.\n- **Mouth ulcers:** citrus can sting existing ulcers.\n- **Skin reactions:** lemon juice on the skin followed by sun exposure can cause a burn-like reaction called phytophotodermatitis. Wash your hands after squeezing lemons outdoors.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- People with **sensitive teeth, enamel erosion** or many fillings\n- People with **acid reflux** or stomach ulcers\n- People with **mouth ulcers**\n- Children, whose enamel is thinner, should have lemon drinks only occasionally and with meals\n\n## When to see a doctor or dentist\n\n- **See your dentist** if you notice tooth sensitivity, changes in colour or shape, or rough or thin edges on your teeth.\n- **See a doctor** if you have **persistent heartburn**, difficulty swallowing, or **signs of a kidney stone**: severe pain in the side or back that comes in waves, blood in the urine, fever or vomiting.\n- If you feel **constantly thirsty** despite drinking plenty, or pass large amounts of urine, see a doctor to check for diabetes.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is warm or cold lemon water better?\n\nThere is no evidence that the temperature changes any health effect. Choose whichever you prefer.\n\n### Can lemon water replace my morning coffee?\n\nIt won't give you caffeine, but some people find it a refreshing start to the day. If you drink both, have them with breakfast rather than sipping over hours to protect your teeth.\n\n### Is bottled lemon juice as good as fresh?\n\nIt works the same way for flavour and citrate, though it may contain preservatives and slightly less vitamin C.\n\n### How much water should I drink?\n\nNeeds vary with climate, activity and health. In hot weather, drink enough that your urine is pale yellow. People with heart or kidney conditions should follow their doctor's advice on fluid.\n\n## References\n\n- Zero DT, Lussi A. Erosion: chemical and biological factors of importance to the dental practitioner. *International Dental Journal*, 2005.\n- Penniston KL, et al. Quantitative assessment of citric acid in lemon juice, lime juice, and commercially-available fruit juice products. *Journal of Endourology*, 2008.\n- NIH Office of Dietary Supplements. Vitamin C: fact sheet for health professionals. https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/\n- NHS. Kidney stones: prevention. https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/kidney-stones/\n"}, {"title": "Magnesium for Sleep and Stress: Does It Help, and Which Type?", "excerpt": "Magnesium supplements are hugely popular for sleep. The evidence is thin, mostly from small studies in older adults. Here's what we know, the safe dose, and who should avoid it.", "category": "sleep-and-stress", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "A few small trials, mostly in older adults, suggest magnesium may slightly improve how quickly people fall asleep; overall evidence quality is low, and it is most likely to help people with low magnesium intake.", "tags": "magnesium, sleep, stress", "slug": "magnesium-for-sleep-and-stress-which-type-is-best", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nMagnesium is an essential mineral involved in nerve and muscle function. Small studies, mostly in older adults, suggest magnesium supplements might help people fall asleep a little faster, but a 2021 systematic review rated the evidence as **low quality** and the benefit as uncertain. People most likely to benefit are those who don't get enough magnesium from their diet.\n\nMagnesium supplements are safe for most healthy adults at sensible doses. Too much causes diarrhoea, and people with kidney disease should not take them without medical advice.\n\n## Why magnesium is linked to sleep\n\nMagnesium plays a role in calming the nervous system. It helps regulate GABA, the brain's main calming chemical messenger, and dampens the activity of excitatory pathways. It is also involved in muscle relaxation and in the body's stress-hormone system.\n\nLow magnesium levels have been linked in observational studies to poorer sleep and higher stress and anxiety scores. However, an association does not prove that taking a supplement will help someone whose magnesium levels are already adequate.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Sleep\n\n- A 2021 systematic review in *BMC Complementary Medicine and Therapies* looked at randomised trials of magnesium for insomnia in older adults. Magnesium shortened the time to fall asleep by about 17 minutes compared with placebo in pooled results, but there were only three small trials and the evidence was rated low quality.\n- One frequently cited trial in elderly people with insomnia found that 500 mg of magnesium a day for eight weeks improved several sleep measures.\n- Trials in younger adults are scarce, and studies combining magnesium with melatonin or zinc make it hard to tell which ingredient helped.\n\n### Stress and anxiety\n\nReviews suggest magnesium might help mild anxiety, particularly in people with low magnesium, but the studies are small and of variable quality.\n\n### Muscle cramps\n\nDespite popular belief, a Cochrane review found that magnesium is unlikely to prevent typical night-time leg cramps in older adults.\n\n## Which type of magnesium is best?\n\nSupplements combine magnesium with another compound, which affects how well it is absorbed and how likely it is to upset the stomach:\n\n- **Magnesium glycinate (bisglycinate):** well absorbed and gentle on the stomach. Often marketed for sleep, though there is no direct evidence it works better for sleep than other forms.\n- **Magnesium citrate:** well absorbed, but has a mild laxative effect.\n- **Magnesium oxide:** cheap and contains a lot of magnesium per tablet, but is poorly absorbed and more likely to cause diarrhoea. It was used in some sleep trials.\n- **Magnesium threonate:** marketed for the brain; human evidence is very limited.\n\nIn short, choose a form that agrees with your stomach. Glycinate or citrate are good starting points.\n\n## How to use it\n\n1. **Start with food.** Good sources include pumpkin seeds, almonds, cashews, spinach, black beans, whole grains, dark chocolate and avocado. The recommended daily intake for adults is about 310 to 420 mg, depending on age and sex.\n2. **If you take a supplement**, a typical dose is **100 to 350 mg of elemental magnesium** in the evening. Check the label for \"elemental\" magnesium, which is the actual amount of the mineral.\n3. **Don't exceed 350 mg a day from supplements** without medical advice. This is the upper limit set by the US National Institutes of Health for adults; it does not include magnesium from food.\n4. **Give it 2 to 4 weeks**, alongside good sleep habits, and stop if you notice no difference.\n\n> Magnesium can reduce the absorption of some medicines. Take thyroid medication (levothyroxine), osteoporosis medicines (bisphosphonates) and antibiotics such as tetracyclines or ciprofloxacin at least two hours before or four to six hours after magnesium.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n- **Digestive upset:** diarrhoea, cramping and nausea are common at higher doses, especially with oxide and citrate.\n- **Too much magnesium:** very high intakes, particularly in people with kidney problems, can cause low blood pressure, muscle weakness, confusion and irregular heartbeat.\n- **Interactions:** besides the medicines above, some diuretics and acid-reducing medicines (proton pump inhibitors) can change magnesium levels.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People with **kidney disease**, who may not be able to clear excess magnesium\n- People with **heart block** or myasthenia gravis, unless supervised\n- Anyone taking the interacting medicines above should plan timing with their pharmacist\n- Pregnant women should use supplements only on a doctor's advice\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- Sleep problems have lasted **more than three months** or are affecting your daytime functioning\n- You **snore loudly** or wake up gasping, which can be signs of sleep apnoea\n- Stress or anxiety is affecting your work, relationships or mood most days\n- You have symptoms that could point to low magnesium or other conditions: muscle twitches, tremors, irregular heartbeat or unusual weakness\n- You take diuretics or proton pump inhibitors long term and have symptoms of low magnesium; a blood test may be needed\n\nCognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the most effective long-term treatment for chronic insomnia, and it is worth asking about.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### When should I take magnesium for sleep?\n\nMost people take it about an hour before bed. There is no strong evidence that timing matters, but taking it with an evening meal may reduce stomach upset.\n\n### Can I take magnesium every night?\n\nDaily use within the recommended limits is generally safe for healthy adults. If you have kidney problems or take regular medicines, check with your doctor first.\n\n### Will magnesium make me drowsy the next day?\n\nIt is not a sedative and usually doesn't cause morning grogginess.\n\n### Do magnesium sprays and bath salts work?\n\nThere is little evidence that magnesium is absorbed in meaningful amounts through the skin. A warm bath before bed may help you relax regardless.\n\n## References\n\n- NIH Office of Dietary Supplements. Magnesium: fact sheet for health professionals. https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Magnesium-HealthProfessional/\n- Mah J, Pitre T. Oral magnesium supplementation for insomnia in older adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *BMC Complementary Medicine and Therapies*, 2021.\n- Abbasi B, et al. The effect of magnesium supplementation on primary insomnia in elderly: a double-blind placebo-controlled clinical trial. *Journal of Research in Medical Sciences*, 2012.\n- Garrison SR, et al. Magnesium for skeletal muscle cramps. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*, 2020.\n"}, {"title": "Natural Remedies for Bloating: What Helps and What Doesn't", "excerpt": "Most bloating comes from trapped gas, slow digestion or certain foods. These are the approaches with the best evidence, and the warning signs that bloating needs checking.", "category": "digestive-health", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Peppermint oil and a supervised low-FODMAP diet have good evidence for bloating linked to irritable bowel syndrome; habits like eating slowly and walking after meals are low-risk and sensible.", "tags": "bloating, gas, IBS", "slug": "natural-remedies-for-bloating-simple-tips-that-may-help", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nOccasional bloating is common and usually harmless. It is often caused by swallowed air, gas made by gut bacteria as they ferment certain foods, constipation, or a sensitive gut. The approaches with the most evidence are:\n\n- **Peppermint oil capsules** for bloating and pain linked to irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)\n- **A low-FODMAP diet**, ideally guided by a dietitian, for people whose bloating is triggered by fermentable carbohydrates\n- **Treating constipation**, which is one of the most common hidden causes\n- **Simple habits**: eating slowly, avoiding fizzy drinks and chewing gum, and walking after meals\n\nBloating that is new, persistent, getting worse, or comes with weight loss, bleeding or a change in bowel habit needs to be checked by a doctor.\n\n## Why bloating happens\n\nBloating means a feeling of fullness, pressure or tightness in the abdomen. Sometimes the belly visibly swells (distension). Common causes include:\n\n- **Swallowed air** from eating quickly, talking while eating, drinking through straws, fizzy drinks and chewing gum\n- **Gas from fermentation.** Some carbohydrates are not fully absorbed in the small intestine and are fermented by bacteria in the large intestine. Beans, lentils, onions, garlic, wheat, some fruits (apples, pears, mango) and sugar alcohols like sorbitol are common culprits.\n- **Constipation**, which slows the movement of gas and stool\n- **IBS**, where the gut is more sensitive to normal amounts of gas and stretch\n- **Lactose intolerance**, where milk sugar is poorly digested\n- **Hormonal changes** before a period\n- **Less common medical causes**, such as coeliac disease, ovarian problems or, rarely, bowel or ovarian cancer\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Peppermint oil\n\nPeppermint oil relaxes the smooth muscle of the gut. Meta-analyses of randomised trials in IBS have found that enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules improve overall symptoms and abdominal pain compared with placebo, and the American College of Gastroenterology's IBS guideline suggests it as a treatment option. Bloating specifically improves in some trials, though less consistently than pain.\n\n### The low-FODMAP diet\n\nFODMAPs are a group of fermentable carbohydrates. Trials in people with IBS show that a low-FODMAP diet reduces bloating, wind and pain for many people. It is a three-step process: a short, strict elimination (usually 2 to 6 weeks), gradual reintroduction to find personal triggers, and then a long-term, less restrictive diet. It is quite complicated, which is why dietitian support is recommended, and it is not meant to be followed strictly for the long term.\n\n### Fibre: helpful, but in the right form\n\nSoluble fibre such as psyllium (ispaghula) helps constipation and IBS symptoms. Insoluble fibre such as wheat bran can make bloating worse for some people. If you increase fibre, do it gradually over a few weeks and drink plenty of water.\n\n### Probiotics\n\nResults are mixed. Some specific strains have reduced bloating in trials, but effects vary by product, and major guidelines do not make a strong recommendation. If you try one, give it about four weeks and stop if there is no improvement.\n\n### Popular remedies with little evidence\n\n- **Fennel, ginger and chamomile teas** are traditional and low-risk, and some people find them soothing, but there is little trial evidence for bloating.\n- **Activated charcoal** has inconsistent results and can interfere with medicines.\n- **Simethicone**, an over-the-counter anti-gas medicine, has limited evidence but is very safe.\n- **\"Detox\" or \"flat tummy\" teas** often contain laxatives such as senna and are not a good idea for regular use.\n\n## Practical steps to try\n\n1. **Slow down at meals.** Chew thoroughly and avoid talking while chewing.\n2. **Cut back on fizzy drinks, chewing gum and sugar-free sweets** containing sorbitol, xylitol or mannitol.\n3. **Walk for 10 to 15 minutes after meals.** Gentle movement helps gas pass through the gut.\n4. **Keep a two-week food and symptom diary.** Note meals, symptoms, bowel movements and, for women, your menstrual cycle. Patterns often become clear.\n5. **Tackle constipation.** Increase fluids, add soluble fibre gradually, and stay active.\n6. **Try reducing common triggers one at a time**, such as onions and garlic, beans, or large amounts of milk, rather than cutting out many foods at once.\n7. **Consider peppermint oil capsules** if you have IBS-type symptoms, after checking with a pharmacist.\n\n> Avoid cutting out whole food groups for long periods without advice, especially for children. A dietitian can help you find triggers without missing important nutrients.\n\n## Side effects and cautions\n\n- **Peppermint oil** can cause heartburn and should be avoided by people with reflux (GORD). Enteric-coated capsules reduce this risk. Do not chew the capsules.\n- **Low-FODMAP diets** can reduce fibre intake and alter gut bacteria if followed strictly for too long.\n- **Probiotics** are generally safe but should be avoided by people who are seriously ill or have weakened immune systems unless a doctor advises them.\n- **Laxatives** in \"detox\" teas can cause cramping, diarrhoea and, with long-term use, dependence.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- People with reflux or a hiatus hernia (peppermint oil)\n- Anyone with a history of an eating disorder, for whom restrictive diets can be harmful\n- Pregnant women: bloating is common in pregnancy, but check with your midwife or doctor before taking supplements\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor promptly if bloating comes with any of these:\n\n- Bloating that is **persistent or happens most days for three weeks or more**, especially if you are over 50, or if you are a woman, because persistent bloating can be an early sign of ovarian cancer\n- **Unexplained weight loss**, loss of appetite or feeling full very quickly\n- **Blood in your stool**, black stools, or bleeding from the bottom\n- A **change in bowel habit** that lasts more than a few weeks\n- **Vomiting**, difficulty swallowing, or severe or worsening pain\n- A **lump** you can feel in your abdomen\n- A family history of bowel or ovarian cancer, or coeliac disease\n\nGet urgent help if you have severe abdominal pain, a swollen, hard abdomen with vomiting, or you cannot pass wind or stool.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why am I bloated every evening?\n\nBloating often builds through the day as you eat and as gas is produced. Large evening meals, eating late, and trigger foods at dinner can make it worse. A food diary can help find the pattern.\n\n### Can drinking water help bloating?\n\nIf constipation is part of the problem, drinking enough water helps keep stools soft. Water itself does not cause bloating, though gulping large amounts with meals can add swallowed air.\n\n### Is bloating a sign of a food intolerance?\n\nSometimes. Lactose intolerance and sensitivity to FODMAPs are common. Coeliac disease, an immune reaction to gluten, should be tested for before cutting out gluten, because the test is only accurate while you are still eating it.\n\n### Should I take a probiotic?\n\nYou can try one for about four weeks. If you notice no difference, there is little point continuing.\n\n## References\n\n- NHS. Bloating. https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/bloating/\n- Lacy BE, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome. *American Journal of Gastroenterology*, 2021.\n- Alammar N, et al. The impact of peppermint oil on the irritable bowel syndrome: a meta-analysis of the pooled clinical data. *BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine*, 2019.\n- National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK). Gas in the digestive tract. https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/gas-digestive-tract\n- National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE). Suspected cancer: recognition and referral, guideline NG12.\n"}, {"title": "Peppermint for Digestion: Tea, Oil Capsules and Cautions", "excerpt": "Peppermint oil capsules have good evidence for irritable bowel syndrome. Peppermint tea is gentler and less studied. Here's how they differ and who should avoid them.", "category": "digestive-health", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules improve IBS symptoms in several trials and are suggested in gastroenterology guidelines; peppermint tea is pleasant but has little direct evidence.", "tags": "peppermint, IBS, indigestion", "slug": "peppermint-tea-for-digestion-benefits-uses-and-cautions", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nPeppermint is one of the few herbal remedies recommended in gastroenterology guidelines, but the evidence is for **enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules** in people with **irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)**. These capsules can reduce abdominal pain and overall IBS symptoms.\n\n**Peppermint tea** contains much less of the active oil. It is a pleasant, caffeine-free drink that many people find settling after meals, but there is little direct research on it. Peppermint in any form can make **heartburn worse**, so it is not a good choice if reflux is your main problem.\n\n## How peppermint works on the gut\n\nThe main active ingredient in peppermint oil is **menthol**. It blocks calcium channels in the smooth muscle of the intestine, which helps the gut wall relax. That can ease cramping, spasm and pain. Peppermint may also reduce the sensitivity of pain nerves in the gut and has mild effects on gut bacteria.\n\nThe same relaxing effect is the downside: peppermint can also relax the valve (lower oesophageal sphincter) between the stomach and food pipe, allowing acid to rise and cause heartburn.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Peppermint oil for IBS\n\nIBS causes recurring abdominal pain with diarrhoea, constipation or both. Several meta-analyses of randomised controlled trials have found that peppermint oil capsules improve global IBS symptoms and abdominal pain more than placebo, typically over 2 to 12 weeks. The American College of Gastroenterology's 2021 IBS guideline suggests peppermint oil for overall IBS symptom relief, while noting the evidence quality is low to moderate.\n\nA larger, well-designed Dutch trial published in 2020 had more mixed results: peppermint oil did not meet its main target but did improve some secondary measures such as abdominal pain and discomfort. So peppermint oil helps some people with IBS, not everyone.\n\n### Indigestion (functional dyspepsia)\n\nCombinations of peppermint oil and caraway oil have reduced indigestion symptoms in some trials, mainly in Europe. Evidence for peppermint alone in indigestion is limited.\n\n### Peppermint tea\n\nThere are very few trials of peppermint tea for digestion. It is reasonable to think it might help a little with cramping or nausea for some people, but the dose of menthol in a cup of tea is much lower and more variable than in capsules.\n\n## How to use peppermint\n\n### Peppermint oil capsules (for IBS)\n\n- Use **enteric-coated** capsules. The coating stops the oil being released in the stomach, which reduces heartburn, so it is released in the intestine where it is needed.\n- Typical doses in trials were **0.2 to 0.4 ml (about 180 to 225 mg) two or three times a day**, taken 30 to 60 minutes before meals. Follow the product label.\n- **Swallow whole.** Do not chew, crush or open the capsules.\n- Avoid taking them at the same time as antacids or acid-reducing medicines, which can dissolve the coating early. Leave about two hours between them.\n- Give it 2 to 4 weeks. If there is no improvement, it is probably not for you.\n\n### Peppermint tea\n\n- Pour just-boiled water over a tea bag or 1 to 2 teaspoons of dried leaves, or a small handful of fresh leaves.\n- Cover and steep for 5 to 10 minutes.\n- Drink after meals, up to three or four cups a day.\n\n> If you have heartburn or acid reflux, peppermint is likely to make it worse. Try ginger or fennel tea instead, and see a doctor if reflux is frequent.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\n**Peppermint oil capsules:**\n\n- Heartburn and reflux (less common with enteric-coated capsules)\n- Nausea, dry mouth, belching\n- A burning sensation around the anus, as some oil passes through\n- Rarely, allergic reactions\n\n**Interactions:**\n\n- Antacids, H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors (such as omeprazole) can make enteric-coated capsules dissolve too early\n- Peppermint oil may affect the breakdown of ciclosporin and some other medicines processed by the liver\n- Peppermint oil may reduce iron absorption when taken at the same time\n\n**Peppermint tea** is very safe for most people in normal amounts.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- People with **reflux, heartburn or a hiatus hernia**\n- People with **gallstones** or bile duct problems should check with a doctor before using peppermint oil\n- **Babies and young children:** never apply peppermint oil or menthol near a child's face, because it can cause breathing problems. Do not give peppermint oil capsules to children without medical advice.\n- **Pregnancy:** peppermint tea in normal amounts is generally considered fine. Avoid concentrated oil capsules unless your doctor agrees.\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nIBS should be diagnosed by a doctor, because other conditions can cause similar symptoms. See a doctor if:\n\n- You have **new bowel symptoms**, especially if you are **over 50**\n- There is **blood in your stool**, black stools, or bleeding from the bottom\n- You have **unexplained weight loss**, fever or night sweats\n- You have **diarrhoea that wakes you at night**\n- You have a family history of bowel cancer, inflammatory bowel disease or coeliac disease\n- You have difficulty swallowing, persistent vomiting, or indigestion with weight loss\n- Pain is severe, constant or getting worse\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is peppermint tea as good as peppermint oil capsules?\n\nNo. Capsules deliver a larger, consistent dose to the intestine. Tea is gentler and less studied, but some people still find it soothing.\n\n### Can I drink peppermint tea every day?\n\nYes, for most people, a few cups a day is fine. Stop if it causes heartburn.\n\n### Does peppermint help with bloating?\n\nIt may help bloating linked to IBS by easing gut spasm. Evidence for general bloating is limited.\n\n### Is spearmint the same as peppermint?\n\nNo. Spearmint contains much less menthol and has not been studied for IBS in the same way.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Peppermint oil. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/peppermint-oil\n- Lacy BE, et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome. *American Journal of Gastroenterology*, 2021.\n- Alammar N, et al. The impact of peppermint oil on the irritable bowel syndrome: a meta-analysis of the pooled clinical data. *BMC Complementary and Alternative Medicine*, 2019.\n- Weerts ZZRM, et al. Efficacy and safety of peppermint oil in a randomized, double-blind trial of patients with irritable bowel syndrome. *Gastroenterology*, 2020.\n"}, {"title": "Salt Water Gargle for Sore Throat: How to Do It and When It Helps", "excerpt": "Gargling warm salt water is a cheap, simple way to ease a sore throat. Here's the right recipe, what the research says, and the signs that a sore throat needs a doctor.", "category": "immunity-and-colds", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Gargling is recommended by health services for sore throat comfort, and a few trials suggest regular gargling may reduce or shorten colds, but evidence is limited.", "tags": "sore throat, gargle, colds", "slug": "salt-water-gargle-for-sore-throat-simple-remedy-and-safety-t", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nGargling with warm salt water is a simple, inexpensive way to soothe a sore throat. The NHS includes it in its self-care advice for adults with a sore throat. The relief is temporary, and gargling does not cure the infection, but it can make swallowing more comfortable.\n\nA few studies suggest regular gargling (with water or salt water) might reduce the chance of getting colds or shorten them slightly, but this research is limited. Gargling is not suitable for young children, who may swallow the water.\n\n## Why it might help\n\nSalt water may ease a sore throat in a few ways:\n\n- **Drawing fluid out of swollen tissue.** A salt solution can pull excess fluid from inflamed throat tissue, which may reduce swelling and discomfort for a while.\n- **Loosening mucus** so it is easier to clear.\n- **Rinsing away irritants** and some of the viruses and bacteria on the surface of the throat.\n- **Warmth** itself is soothing for many people.\n\n## What the research says\n\n- A 2005 Japanese randomised trial of nearly 400 healthy adults found that people who gargled plain water three times a day had about a **third fewer upper respiratory infections** over 60 days than those who didn't gargle. Gargling with an antiseptic solution didn't do better than water.\n- A 2019 Scottish pilot trial (ELVIS) found that people who started **salt-water nasal rinsing and gargling** early in a cold had colds that were about two days shorter, used fewer medicines, and were less likely to pass the infection on to household members. This was a small study and needs confirmation.\n- There are few trials of salt-water gargling for sore throat pain specifically, but it is low-risk and widely recommended for comfort.\n\n## How to gargle with salt water\n\n1. **Mix the solution.** Dissolve **half a teaspoon of salt** (about 2.5 to 3 grams) in **one cup (about 240 ml) of warm water**. The water should be comfortably warm, not hot.\n2. **Take a mouthful**, tilt your head back, and gargle for about **30 seconds**, making an \"ahh\" sound to move the water around the back of your throat.\n3. **Spit it out.** Don't swallow it.\n4. **Repeat** with the rest of the cup.\n5. **Do this several times a day**, for example every 3 to 4 hours, as needed.\n\nOther things that help a sore throat:\n\n- Drink plenty of fluids, especially warm drinks and cool water\n- Suck lozenges, ice chips or ice lollies (adults and older children)\n- Take paracetamol or ibuprofen if needed, following the label\n- A spoonful of honey for adults and children over one year\n- Rest and avoid smoking or smoky environments\n\n> Gargling is not suitable for young children, who may swallow the salt water. Most children can learn to gargle safely around age 6 to 8. Try it with plain water first.\n\n## Possible side effects\n\nSalt-water gargling is very safe for most people. Possible issues:\n\n- **Swallowing too much salt water** can cause nausea and, rarely, is a concern for people who need to limit salt (such as some people with heart failure or high blood pressure). Spit the water out.\n- **Using too much salt** can irritate or dry the throat. Stick to half a teaspoon per cup.\n- **Very hot water** can scald the mouth and throat.\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- **Young children** who cannot gargle and spit reliably\n- People who have trouble swallowing or a high risk of choking\n- People on a strict salt-restricted diet should be especially careful to spit out the solution\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nMost sore throats are caused by viruses and get better within a week without antibiotics. See a doctor if:\n\n- Your sore throat **lasts more than a week** or keeps coming back\n- You have a **high fever** (above 38.5 °C), or fever lasting more than three days\n- You have **white patches or pus on the tonsils** with fever and swollen, tender neck glands, especially without a cough, which can be signs of strep throat that may need antibiotics\n- You have a **weakened immune system**, for example due to chemotherapy or certain medicines\n- You develop a **rash** with your sore throat\n- Pain is mostly on **one side**, with difficulty opening your mouth or a muffled voice, which can be a sign of an abscess\n\n**Get emergency help** if you or your child has:\n\n- **Difficulty breathing**, noisy breathing, or drooling and unable to swallow saliva\n- Severe swelling of the throat or tongue\n- A stiff neck with fever and headache\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How often should I gargle with salt water?\n\nEvery three to four hours while your throat is sore is reasonable. There's no strong evidence for a specific number of times.\n\n### Is sea salt better than table salt?\n\nNo. Any salt works. Iodised table salt is fine.\n\n### Can I add baking soda or honey?\n\nSome people add a quarter teaspoon of baking soda, which is generally safe. Honey is better taken by mouth than gargled, as it's swallowed to coat the throat.\n\n### Does salt water kill bacteria in the throat?\n\nSalt water may reduce the number of germs on the surface briefly, but it doesn't cure an infection. Strep throat, for example, may need antibiotics.\n\n## References\n\n- NHS. Sore throat. https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/sore-throat/\n- Satomura K, et al. Prevention of upper respiratory tract infections by gargling: a randomized trial. *American Journal of Preventive Medicine*, 2005.\n- Ramalingam S, et al. A pilot, open labelled, randomised controlled trial of hypertonic saline nasal irrigation and gargling for the common cold. *Scientific Reports*, 2019.\n- National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE). Sore throat (acute): antimicrobial prescribing, guideline NG84. https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng84\n"}, {"title": "Turmeric for Inflammation: Benefits, Safety and Who Should Avoid It", "excerpt": "Turmeric and its compound curcumin are sold for almost every kind of inflammation. The best evidence is for knee osteoarthritis pain, and supplements carry risks most people don't hear about.", "category": "everyday-wellness", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Small trials suggest curcumin extracts may ease knee osteoarthritis pain modestly, but evidence for other inflammatory conditions is weak, and concentrated supplements have been linked to liver injury.", "tags": "turmeric, curcumin, inflammation", "slug": "turmeric-for-inflammation-benefits-safety-and-who-should-avo", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nTurmeric is a safe and flavourful spice in food. As a supplement, its main active compound, **curcumin**, has been studied for many inflammatory conditions. The most consistent finding is a **modest reduction in pain from knee osteoarthritis**, roughly similar in some small trials to standard anti-inflammatory painkillers. For most other claims, including weight loss, depression, heart disease and cancer, the evidence is weak or missing.\n\nTurmeric supplements are not risk-free. Concentrated products, especially those combined with black pepper extract to boost absorption, have been linked to rare cases of liver injury, and they can interact with blood thinners and diabetes medicines.\n\n## What turmeric and curcumin are\n\nTurmeric is the bright yellow root of *Curcuma longa*, a plant in the ginger family, used for centuries in South Asian and Middle Eastern cooking and in traditional medicine. Curcumin makes up only about 3% of turmeric powder by weight.\n\nCurcumin has anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects in laboratory studies. The problem is that the body absorbs very little of it: most is broken down in the gut and liver. That is why supplements often contain **piperine** (from black pepper) or use special formulations to increase absorption. Better absorption may increase benefits, but it may also increase side effects.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Knee osteoarthritis\n\nThis is where the evidence is strongest. Several meta-analyses of randomised trials have found that turmeric or curcumin extracts reduce knee pain and stiffness compared with placebo over 4 to 12 weeks. A few small trials compared curcumin with ibuprofen or diclofenac and found similar pain relief with fewer stomach side effects.\n\nThe limits are important: most trials were small, short and of mixed quality, used different products and doses, and some were funded by supplement makers. The benefit, where seen, is modest.\n\n### Other conditions\n\n- **Rheumatoid arthritis and inflammatory bowel disease:** a few small trials show possible benefit when curcumin is added to standard treatment, but the evidence is too limited to recommend it, and it should never replace prescribed medicine.\n- **Exercise soreness:** some studies suggest less muscle soreness, with inconsistent results.\n- **Depression, heart disease, diabetes, cancer, dementia:** promising lab and animal studies have not translated into convincing human evidence.\n\nThe US National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health summarises it this way: there is little reliable evidence to support turmeric for any health condition, because few high-quality studies have been done.\n\n## How people use it\n\n- **In food:** turmeric in curries, rice, soups and golden milk is safe for almost everyone. Food amounts are too small to match the doses used in trials, but there is no harm in enjoying it.\n- **As a supplement:** knee osteoarthritis trials commonly used around 500 to 1,500 mg of curcumin extract a day, split into doses, for at least 4 to 8 weeks.\n\nIf you want to try a supplement for joint pain:\n\n1. Talk to your doctor or pharmacist first, especially if you take any regular medicines.\n2. Choose a product from a reputable manufacturer, ideally independently tested for quality (for example by USP or a similar body). Contamination with lead and other substances has been found in some turmeric products.\n3. Give it a fair trial of 8 to 12 weeks, then stop if you notice no difference.\n4. Stop immediately and see a doctor if you develop any signs of liver problems (see below).\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nCommon, usually mild side effects of supplements include stomach upset, nausea, diarrhoea and acid reflux.\n\nMore serious concerns:\n\n- **Liver injury.** The US LiverTox database and regulators in Australia and Europe have reported cases of liver injury linked to turmeric and curcumin supplements, many involving high-absorption products with piperine. Most people recovered after stopping, but some cases were serious.\n- **Bleeding risk.** Curcumin may slow blood clotting. Combining it with warfarin, apixaban, clopidogrel, aspirin or similar medicines may raise bleeding risk. Stop supplements two weeks before planned surgery.\n- **Diabetes medicines.** Curcumin may lower blood sugar slightly, adding to the effect of diabetes medicines.\n- **Gallbladder problems.** Turmeric can make the gallbladder contract, which may trigger pain in people with gallstones or bile duct problems.\n- **Iron absorption.** High doses may reduce iron absorption, which matters for people with iron deficiency.\n- **Other medicines.** Piperine affects how the liver processes many drugs, so products containing it may change medicine levels.\n\n> Stop turmeric supplements and contact a doctor if you notice yellowing of the skin or eyes, dark urine, pale stools, itching, unusual tiredness or pain in the upper right of your abdomen.\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\nAvoid turmeric or curcumin **supplements** (normal food amounts are fine) if you:\n\n- Have liver disease, or have had liver problems with a medicine or supplement before\n- Take blood thinners or antiplatelet medicines, or have a bleeding disorder\n- Have gallstones or bile duct obstruction\n- Are pregnant or breastfeeding (safety of supplement doses is not established)\n- Have surgery planned within two weeks\n- Take diabetes medicines, unless your doctor agrees and you monitor your blood sugar\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nTurmeric should not delay proper assessment. See a doctor if:\n\n- A joint is **hot, red and swollen**, or you have a fever with joint pain, which can be a sign of infection or gout\n- Joint pain is getting worse, waking you at night, or limiting your daily activities\n- You have **morning stiffness lasting more than an hour** or several swollen joints, which can suggest inflammatory arthritis that needs early treatment\n- You have any of the liver warning signs listed above\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does turmeric need black pepper to work?\n\nBlack pepper's piperine increases curcumin absorption many times over. That is why it is added to supplements. In food, cooking turmeric with a little fat and black pepper is a common and harmless approach. In supplements, higher absorption also means higher potential for side effects and interactions.\n\n### Is golden milk good for inflammation?\n\nGolden milk is a pleasant drink, but a typical cup contains far less curcumin than the doses used in studies. Enjoy it as food rather than as a treatment.\n\n### Can turmeric replace ibuprofen?\n\nA few small trials suggest similar pain relief for knee osteoarthritis, but the evidence is not strong enough to recommend switching. Talk to your doctor about the safest long-term pain plan for you.\n\n### Is turmeric safe for teeth?\n\nTurmeric can stain teeth, dentures and fillings yellow temporarily. Rinsing with water after turmeric drinks and regular brushing usually prevents this.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Turmeric. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/turmeric\n- Daily JW, Yang M, Park S. Efficacy of turmeric extracts and curcumin for alleviating the symptoms of joint arthritis: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized clinical trials. *Journal of Medicinal Food*, 2016.\n- National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases. LiverTox: Clinical and Research Information on Drug-Induced Liver Injury. Turmeric.\n- Therapeutic Goods Administration (Australia). Medicines containing turmeric or curcumin: risk of liver injury, safety advisory.\n"}, {"title": "Valerian Root for Sleep: Benefits, Side Effects and Cautions", "excerpt": "Valerian is one of the most popular herbal sleep aids, but studies are inconsistent. Here's what the evidence shows, how it's taken, and who should avoid it.", "category": "sleep-and-stress", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Reviews find some people report better sleep with valerian, but objective sleep measures rarely improve and study quality is mixed, so its effect is uncertain.", "tags": "valerian, sleep, insomnia", "slug": "valerian-root-for-sleep-benefits-side-effects-and-cautions", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nValerian root has been used as a sleep aid since ancient Greece and is still one of the best-selling herbal sleep supplements. Research results are **inconsistent**: some studies found people felt they slept better, but when sleep was measured objectively in a sleep lab, valerian usually made little difference. The US National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health describes the evidence as inconclusive.\n\nValerian is generally safe for short-term use in healthy adults, but it can cause headaches, dizziness and morning grogginess, and it should not be combined with alcohol or other sedatives.\n\n## What valerian is\n\nValerian (*Valeriana officinalis*) is a flowering plant whose roots have a strong, earthy smell that many people find unpleasant. The root contains valerenic acid, valepotriates and other compounds that may increase the activity of **GABA**, the brain's main calming messenger. Products vary widely in how they are made and how much of these compounds they contain, which is one reason studies disagree.\n\n## What the research says\n\n- A 2006 meta-analysis in the *American Journal of Medicine* found that valerian might improve sleep quality, but noted significant evidence of publication bias (positive studies being more likely to be published).\n- A 2010 meta-analysis in *Sleep Medicine* found that people taking valerian reported better sleep quality, but **objective measures** of sleep, such as time to fall asleep and total sleep time, did not improve significantly.\n- Some studies suggest valerian needs to be taken nightly for **two to four weeks** before any effect appears, rather than working on the first night like a sleeping pill.\n- Studies combining valerian with hops or lemon balm have shown some benefit, but it's hard to know which ingredient is responsible.\n\nOverall, valerian may help some people feel they sleep better, but it is not a reliable treatment for insomnia.\n\n## How people use it\n\n- **Typical doses in studies:** 300 to 600 mg of valerian root extract, taken 30 minutes to 2 hours before bed. Follow the product label.\n- **Valerian tea:** 2 to 3 grams of dried root steeped in hot water for 10 to 15 minutes. The taste is strong and earthy.\n- **Give it time:** if you try it, take it nightly for 2 to 4 weeks before deciding whether it helps.\n- **Short-term only:** most studies lasted 4 to 6 weeks. Long-term safety is not well established.\n- **Choose a quality product:** look for products tested by independent organisations, because content varies.\n\n> Don't drive or operate machinery if valerian makes you drowsy, and don't combine it with alcohol, sleeping tablets, or other sedating medicines.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nValerian is usually well tolerated in short-term use. Possible side effects include:\n\n- Headache, dizziness\n- Stomach upset\n- Morning drowsiness or feeling \"hungover\"\n- Vivid dreams\n- Paradoxically, some people feel restless or have trouble sleeping\n\nRare cases of **liver injury** have been reported, mostly with multi-herb products, so a direct link is uncertain.\n\n**Withdrawal-like symptoms** (such as anxiety and a racing heart) have been reported in a person who stopped very high doses suddenly after long-term use. If you have taken valerian for a long time, reduce it gradually.\n\nInteractions:\n\n- **Alcohol, benzodiazepines, sleeping tablets, opioids, antihistamines and other sedatives:** increased drowsiness\n- **Anaesthesia:** stop valerian about two weeks before surgery, and tell your anaesthetist\n- **Medicines processed by the liver:** valerian may affect some liver enzymes, although studies suggest this effect is small\n\n## Who should avoid it\n\n- **Pregnant or breastfeeding women**, as safety hasn't been established\n- **Children under 3**; older children only on medical advice\n- People with **liver disease**\n- People taking **sedating medicines** or who drink alcohol regularly in the evening\n- People with **upcoming surgery**\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You have had difficulty sleeping at least three nights a week for **three months or more**\n- You feel **very sleepy during the day**, or have fallen asleep while driving\n- You **snore loudly**, gasp or stop breathing during sleep (possible sleep apnoea)\n- You have **restless legs**, pain, frequent urination at night or breathing problems keeping you awake\n- Your sleep problems come with **low mood, anxiety** or other mental health concerns\n\nThe best-supported long-term treatment for chronic insomnia is **cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia (CBT-I)**, which is available through doctors, psychologists and some digital programmes.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is valerian addictive?\n\nIt does not appear to cause dependence like some sleeping pills, but stopping high doses suddenly after long-term use may cause withdrawal symptoms in rare cases. Reduce gradually.\n\n### Can I take valerian with melatonin?\n\nSome products combine them. There is no strong evidence the combination works better, and combining sedating products increases the risk of next-day drowsiness. Check with a pharmacist.\n\n### Why does valerian smell so bad?\n\nThe smell comes from isovaleric acid, which forms as the root dries. Capsules avoid the taste.\n\n### How quickly does valerian work?\n\nSome people notice a difference within days, but studies suggest it may take 2 to 4 weeks of nightly use.\n\n## References\n\n- National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH). Valerian. https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/valerian\n- NIH Office of Dietary Supplements. Valerian: fact sheet for health professionals. https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Valerian-HealthProfessional/\n- Bent S, et al. Valerian for sleep: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *American Journal of Medicine*, 2006.\n- Fernández-San-Martín MI, et al. Effectiveness of valerian on insomnia: a meta-analysis of randomized placebo-controlled trials. *Sleep Medicine*, 2010.\n"}, {"title": "Vitamin D: Benefits, Food Sources and Supplement Safety", "excerpt": "Vitamin D is essential for bones and muscles, and deficiency is surprisingly common even in sunny countries. Here's who needs a supplement, how much is safe, and which claims don't hold up.", "category": "everyday-wellness", "evidence_level": "moderate", "evidence_summary": "Vitamin D clearly prevents and treats deficiency and its bone effects, but large trials found that supplements given to people who are not deficient don't prevent heart disease, cancer or depression.", "tags": "vitamin D, bones, supplements", "slug": "vitamin-d-benefits-food-sources-and-supplement-safety", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nVitamin D helps the body absorb calcium and keeps bones and muscles healthy. Too little causes soft, weak bones (rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults) and contributes to osteoporosis. Correcting deficiency is clearly worthwhile.\n\nBut in people who already have enough vitamin D, large, well-run trials have found that extra supplements **do not prevent** heart disease, cancer, depression or most other conditions they are often marketed for. More is not better, and very high doses can be harmful.\n\n## How the body gets vitamin D\n\n- **Sunlight:** the skin makes vitamin D when exposed to UVB light. This is the main source for most people.\n- **Food:** naturally present in only a few foods: oily fish (salmon, sardines, mackerel), egg yolks, liver and some mushrooms exposed to UV light. Some milks, yoghurts, cereals and plant drinks are fortified.\n- **Supplements:** vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol) or D2 (ergocalciferol). D3 tends to raise blood levels more effectively.\n\n## Why deficiency is common, even in sunny countries\n\nIt surprises many people that vitamin D deficiency is common in the Gulf region, including Oman, despite strong sunshine. Studies across Gulf countries have consistently found high rates of low vitamin D. Reasons include:\n\n- Spending most of the day **indoors** or in cars, especially during hot months\n- **Covering most of the skin** outdoors\n- **Darker skin**, which needs more sun exposure to make the same amount of vitamin D\n- **Sunscreen use**, which is important for skin cancer prevention but reduces vitamin D production\n- Low intake of oily fish and fortified foods\n\nOther groups at higher risk anywhere include older adults, people with obesity, people with conditions that affect fat absorption (such as coeliac or Crohn's disease, or after weight-loss surgery), and breastfed babies.\n\n## What the research says\n\n### Bones and muscles\n\nVitamin D prevents and treats rickets and osteomalacia. In older adults with low levels, vitamin D combined with calcium can reduce fracture risk. In people with normal levels, extra vitamin D does not seem to strengthen bones or prevent falls further.\n\n### Other conditions\n\nThe large US VITAL trial gave 2,000 IU of vitamin D a day or placebo to over 25,000 adults for about five years. Vitamin D did **not reduce** heart attacks, strokes or cancer diagnoses. Similar large trials elsewhere had similar results. There were hints of benefit in some subgroups (for example, a possible reduction in autoimmune disease), but these need confirmation.\n\nFor respiratory infections, some reviews found a small protective effect with daily doses in people with low levels, but results are mixed.\n\n## How much do you need?\n\nUS recommended daily intakes (the NHS gives similar advice):\n\n- **Babies up to 12 months:** 400 IU (10 mcg). Breastfed babies usually need a supplement; formula is already fortified.\n- **Children and adults up to 70:** 600 IU (15 mcg)\n- **Adults over 70:** 800 IU (20 mcg)\n- **Upper limit for adults:** 4,000 IU (100 mcg) a day, unless prescribed by a doctor\n\nPeople with confirmed deficiency are often prescribed higher doses for a limited time, followed by a maintenance dose. This should be guided by a doctor.\n\n### Practical tips\n\n1. **Get some sensible sun.** Short periods of sun on the arms and face, outside the hottest part of the day, help. Avoid burning.\n2. **Eat oily fish** once or twice a week, and choose fortified foods if you don't.\n3. **Consider a daily supplement** of 400 to 1,000 IU if you are in a high-risk group, especially if you rarely go outside or cover most of your skin.\n4. **Take it with a meal containing some fat**, which improves absorption.\n5. **Ask for a blood test** if you have risk factors or symptoms such as bone pain or muscle weakness, rather than guessing with high doses.\n\n> High-dose vitamin D products (10,000 IU, 50,000 IU) are widely sold online. Taking them daily without medical supervision can cause dangerously high calcium levels.\n\n## Side effects of too much vitamin D\n\nVitamin D toxicity almost always comes from supplements, never from sunlight. It raises calcium levels in the blood, which can cause:\n\n- Nausea, vomiting, poor appetite and constipation\n- Excessive thirst and frequent urination\n- Confusion, weakness and tiredness\n- Kidney stones and kidney damage\n- Irregular heartbeat in severe cases\n\n## Interactions and who should be careful\n\n- **Kidney disease, sarcoidosis, some lymphomas, or a history of high calcium or kidney stones:** take only under medical supervision\n- **Thiazide diuretics:** combined with high vitamin D and calcium, can raise calcium levels\n- **Digoxin:** high calcium increases the risk of heart rhythm problems\n- **Steroids, some epilepsy medicines, orlistat and cholestyramine** can lower vitamin D levels; your doctor may recommend a supplement\n- **Pregnancy:** a standard supplement (400 to 600 IU) is commonly recommended; avoid high doses unless prescribed\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nSee a doctor if:\n\n- You have **bone pain, muscle weakness or aches**, frequent fractures, or a child has bowed legs or delayed walking\n- You are in a **high-risk group** and have never been tested\n- You are taking **high-dose vitamin D** and develop thirst, frequent urination, nausea, confusion or weakness\n- You have **kidney disease** or a condition affecting calcium levels\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is it better to take vitamin D daily or weekly?\n\nDaily or weekly doses can both correct low levels. Some research suggests daily doses work more consistently, and very large single doses (such as a big yearly dose) may be less effective or even increase falls in older adults.\n\n### Should I take vitamin D with calcium?\n\nOnly if your diet is low in calcium or your doctor advises it. Most people can get enough calcium from food.\n\n### Does vitamin D help with tiredness?\n\nIf tiredness is caused by vitamin D deficiency, correcting it can help. Tiredness has many causes, though, so it is worth seeing a doctor if it persists.\n\n### Does vitamin D help teeth?\n\nVitamin D supports healthy bones and teeth, and severe deficiency in childhood affects tooth development. It does not replace brushing with fluoride toothpaste and limiting sugar for preventing decay.\n\n## References\n\n- NIH Office of Dietary Supplements. Vitamin D: fact sheet for health professionals. https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminD-HealthProfessional/\n- Manson JE, et al. Vitamin D supplements and prevention of cancer and cardiovascular disease (VITAL). *New England Journal of Medicine*, 2019.\n- NHS. Vitamin D. https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d/\n- Bassil D, et al. Hypovitaminosis D in the Middle East and North Africa: prevalence, risk factors and impact on outcomes. *Dermato-Endocrinology*, 2013.\n"}, {"title": "Zinc for Colds: What to Know Before Taking It", "excerpt": "Zinc lozenges might shorten a cold slightly if started early, but the evidence is uncertain and too much zinc has real downsides. Here's how to use it, and what never to use.", "category": "immunity-and-colds", "evidence_level": "limited", "evidence_summary": "Some trials found zinc lozenges started within a day of symptoms shortened colds, but the most recent Cochrane review rated the evidence as low certainty, with frequent side effects.", "tags": "zinc, colds, immunity", "slug": "zinc-for-colds-what-to-know-before-taking-it", "content": "## Quick answer\n\nZinc is an essential mineral the immune system needs. **Zinc lozenges** started within 24 hours of the first cold symptoms may shorten a cold by about a day or two, according to some trials. However, the latest Cochrane review (2024) found the evidence to be **low certainty** overall, and side effects such as a bad taste and nausea were common.\n\nZinc does not prevent colds in well-nourished people, and taking high doses for weeks can cause copper deficiency. **Zinc nasal sprays and gels** have been linked to permanent loss of smell and should never be used.\n\n## Why zinc might help\n\nZinc is needed for normal immune cell function. In the lab, zinc ions can stop the most common cold viruses (rhinoviruses) from multiplying and from attaching to cells in the nose and throat. The theory behind lozenges is that slowly dissolving zinc in the mouth releases zinc ions directly where the virus is active.\n\n## What the research says\n\n- **Earlier reviews** found that zinc lozenges started within 24 hours reduced the length of colds, with some analyses suggesting a reduction of about a third in adults.\n- **The 2024 Cochrane review**, which included over 30 trials, concluded that zinc may shorten colds slightly but that the evidence was of low certainty, trial designs varied widely, and the benefit was uncertain. Zinc did not clearly prevent colds.\n- **Formulation matters.** Lozenges using zinc acetate or zinc gluconate with no citric acid or other ingredients that bind zinc seemed to work better in some analyses. Many commercial products do not release much free zinc.\n- **Children:** evidence in children is very limited and inconsistent.\n\n## How to use zinc for a cold\n\nIf you want to try zinc lozenges:\n\n1. **Start within 24 hours** of the first symptoms. Starting later is unlikely to help.\n2. **Choose zinc acetate or zinc gluconate lozenges.** Avoid products where citric acid, tartaric acid or similar ingredients appear near the top of the list.\n3. **Let the lozenge dissolve slowly** in your mouth; don't chew or swallow it whole.\n4. **Typical trial doses** were around 75 to 100 mg of elemental zinc a day in total, split into one lozenge every 2 to 3 hours while awake.\n5. **Stop after 5 to 7 days**, or when symptoms clear.\n\nThese doses are above the long-term upper limit for adults (40 mg a day), which is why they should only be used briefly.\n\n**Food sources** of zinc include meat, shellfish (especially oysters), dairy, eggs, pumpkin seeds, nuts, beans and whole grains. Most people eating a varied diet get enough.\n\n> Never use zinc nasal sprays, gels or swabs. Zinc applied inside the nose has been linked to long-lasting or permanent loss of smell. US regulators warned against these products in 2009.\n\n## Possible side effects and interactions\n\nShort-term side effects of lozenges:\n\n- **Bad or metallic taste**, which is very common\n- **Nausea**, especially on an empty stomach\n- Mouth irritation\n\nLong-term high doses (more than 40 mg a day for weeks or months):\n\n- **Copper deficiency**, which can cause anaemia and nerve damage\n- Lower \"good\" HDL cholesterol\n- Weakened immune function\n\nInteractions:\n\n- **Antibiotics**: zinc reduces absorption of tetracyclines (such as doxycycline) and quinolones (such as ciprofloxacin). Take the antibiotic at least 2 hours before or 4 to 6 hours after zinc.\n- **Penicillamine** (for Wilson's disease or rheumatoid arthritis): zinc reduces its absorption\n- **Thiazide diuretics** increase zinc loss in urine\n\n## Who should be careful\n\n- **Children:** use only doses on child-specific products and check with a pharmacist\n- **Pregnant or breastfeeding women:** don't exceed the upper limit without medical advice\n- People taking **antibiotics or penicillamine** (see timing above)\n- People already taking **multivitamins or other zinc supplements**: add up your total\n\n## When to see a doctor\n\nMost colds go away in 7 to 10 days. See a doctor if:\n\n- Symptoms last **more than 10 days** or improve and then get worse again\n- You have a **high fever**, or a fever lasting more than three days\n- You have **difficulty breathing**, chest pain or wheezing\n- You have **severe sinus pain**, ear pain, or a very sore throat with difficulty swallowing\n- You have a long-term condition such as asthma, COPD, heart disease or a weakened immune system and symptoms are worsening\n- You notice a **loss of smell** that does not return after the cold\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can zinc prevent colds?\n\nThere is no good evidence that zinc supplements prevent colds in people who already get enough zinc.\n\n### Is zinc in multivitamins enough?\n\nFor general health, yes. The doses used for treating colds are much higher and short-term.\n\n### Can I take zinc and vitamin C together?\n\nYes. There is no harmful interaction. Vitamin C taken regularly may slightly shorten colds, but starting it after a cold begins has shown little benefit.\n\n### Why does zinc make me nauseous?\n\nZinc irritates the stomach lining, especially on an empty stomach. Lozenges dissolving slowly in the mouth may be easier to tolerate than tablets, and eating something beforehand helps.\n\n## References\n\n- NIH Office of Dietary Supplements. Zinc: fact sheet for health professionals. https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Zinc-HealthProfessional/\n- Nault D, et al. Zinc for prevention and treatment of the common cold. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*, 2024.\n- Hemilä H. Zinc lozenges and the common cold: a meta-analysis comparing zinc acetate and zinc gluconate, and the role of zinc dosage. *JRSM Open*, 2017.\n- US Food and Drug Administration. Warnings on three Zicam intranasal zinc products, 2009.\n"}];
